      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</head>
        <p>PLEASANT PARAGRAPHS.</p>
        <p>Boy: Pa, are despots happy? Pa (impatiently): Ask your mother. "I sAw a manly young person on a bicycle to. day." "Male or female?" WHY did the poet ask the woodman to "spare that tree ?"—Because he thought he was a good feller.</p>
        <p>CURIOUS but short-sighted Gentleman (who has swarmed up a sign-post to read the inscription: Great Scott I Wet paint! "I wiss to see some collars, please." "Yes," madam. Ah—ladies' or gentlemen's?" " Gentle- men's, sir. For ladies' use." BABY-CARRIAGE DEALER (affably) : What sort of a baby-carriage do you wish sir? Wheeler (meekly): W-hy—er—a tandem one. STRICTLY SPEAKING.—Dix —" How long has your cook been with you?" Hicks—" This is the second year of her reign." No Room FOR IT.—Do you and your wife ever have any disputes ? " "No ; we live in a flat, and there is no room for argument." "How did you get along with the French lady ? " asked papa. "Well," said his daughter, "she understood what she said, and I understood what I said."</p>
        <p>TOM: You couldn't lend me five till to-morrow, could you? Dick: What a mind-reader you are? "IT," said the grinning savage, as he turned the machine-gun on the discomfited .civilisers, "is a i</p>
        <p>poor maiim that won't work both ways." SUNDAY CYCLIST : Can you tell me where this road leads to. Janet: Ay ! Laddie ! It just leads straight tae perdition! _. . _</p>
        <p>"YOUR liusband seems to be a victim of the tobacco habit." "No—l'm the victim. He thoroughly enjoys it." "LET'S go to the mountains." "But I have nothing to wear." "Then I suppose we'll have to go to the sea-shore." LADY (who has a sick husband) : "Don't you think, doctor, you ought to bleed my husband?" Doctor (absent-mindedly) : "No, madam, not until he gets well." "GEORGE, I fear you are marrying me just be- cause my uncle left me a fortune." "No, my precious. I'd marry you just the same if any other friend had left it to you."</p>
        <p>PERTINENT.—In Russia teachers are none too well paid. As a scholastic meeting some one pro- posed the toast—" Long live our school teachers." "What on ? " asked a cadaverous-looking speci- men, rising in his seat. " MY wife is all that is good, and she is so easily pleased," said Mr. Simiah. "Yes," said Miss •Verjuice, with one of her most meaning smiles, "I can quite believe that." "OF course I will be homelier some day, dear," she whispered. "Impossible," he replied, gal- lantly, and he marvels that she sent his presents back.</p>
        <p>"You seem to be quite learned on the subject of domestic life," said a widow to a young man; "are you married?" "No," said the callow one, with a chuckle, "but my father is." CREDITOR (determinedly) : "I shall call at your house every week until you pay this account, sir." Debtor (in the blandest of tones) : "Then, sir, there seems every probability of our acquaintance- ship ripening into friendship!" "Do you really wish to make my daughter happy ?" asked old Bullion, of an impecunious suitor. "Certainly, sir." "Then don't marry her," said the old fellow sharply. AN Irish soldier was asked if he had met with much hospitality in India. "Oh, yes," he replied, "too much—l was in hospital nearly all the time."</p>
        <p>AUTOBIOGRAPHICAL. - The self-made man was speaking. He said: "My father was a raiser of hogs. There was a large family of us ; " and then his voice was drowned by the applause. "I FEEL SO miserable again this morning," said a complaining wife. " Yes," replied her exas- perated husband, "you wouldn't be happy if you weren't miserable all the time." _</p>
        <p>TEACHER : Tommy, when was Rome built? Tommy: In the night. Teacher: How came ,you to make such a mistake? Tommy: You said yesterday Rome wasn't built in a day. THE BUTLER—" Hand hevery night at the hour of midnight the ghost happears, and groans, and wrings its hands." American tourist —" Ah, must have died in the cucumber season!" -</p>
        <p>THINKING to stock his depleated larder, a Western editor wrote: "Poultry taken in ex- change for advertisements." A compositor set it up: "Poetry taken, &amp;c." Since that time the office-boy has been clearing a dollar a day from the wastepaper man. SMITH: "See Jones over there laughing so heartily. Somebody must have told him a funny story." Brown: "More likely Jones told it him- self."</p>
        <p>JONES: "What induced your wife to part with her parrot? I thought she used to be so attached to it." Brown: "She was; but it finally got so that it always wanted the last word, and she de- cided to give it up." A REASON.—" Why were you discharged from your last place?" asked the merchant of the applicant for a situation. "I was discharged for good behaviour, sir." "Wasn't that a singular reason for discharge ! " "Well, you see, good behaviour took nine months off my sentence." THERE'S one thing, however, that I can't quite understand," said the Rev. Mr. Choker. "What's that ? " Mrs. Van Scadds inquired. "Why, it is this—Every time I get back to my church after being away upon a vacation the members of the congregation seem to be even better than they were before I left." "Well," the thoughtless lady said, "I suppose it is only natural that the rest should benefit them as well as you." A LESSON IN MORALS. Mother : "Now, Willy, you told me a falsehood. Do you know what happens to little boys who tells falsehoods ? " Willy (sheepishly) : "No." Mother: "Why, a big black man with only one eye in the centre of his forehead catches him and flies with him up to the moon and makes him pick up sticks for the rest of his life. Now, you will never tell a false- hood again, will you? It is dreadfully wicked !" "What," said the visitor to the village of his childhood, "what's become of the one boy I hated —Willie Hawker, the sneak? In prison, no doubt —he bore that fate on his face." "Hush !" said the villager. "He is now Mr. Hawker, the famous millionaire." " What ?" cried the visitor, "my dear old schoolfellow a millionaire. I must call upon him and revive the old friend- ship." Two Scotclimen met on board a steamer going to the United States, but the rough weather was too much for one of them. When they met a week later, Jamie greeted his friend warmly, and proceeded to make him comfortable with a deck chair and some rugs, remarking with a laugh— " Why, Wullie, man, ye're lukin' years older than when I saw ye last." Sma' wunner at that," was the reply; "I've had several berth days, ye ken, since I cam' on board." THE father was urging upon his son, aged fifteen, to devote himself more seriously to his studies. "Distinguish yourself," he said, "and your name will appear in the papers—like these," and he pointed to a list of scholarship winders. "My dear father I" said his son, in a tone of pained remon- strance, "in these days of morbid craving for notoriety, it is surely the first duty of every true gentleman to keep his name out of the papers. You may rely upon me to fulfil that duty." SHE was rather an aggressive looking woman, and when she beckoned to the library attendant he made haste to respond. "I want the com- panion volume to The Descent of Man'," she ex- plained. "The—the what?" asked the attendant. "The companion volume to 'The Descent of Man'," she repeated. " I—l never heard of one," said the attendant, doubtfully. "I don't believe there is any." "Nonsense, young man !" she retorted, looking at him sharplg. "I'm sure there must be something about the rise of woman." A QUAINT old character used to live in a Devon- shire town whose .business caused him to drive about a great deal in a donkey cart. One day a lady was surprised to. see him pulling his cart through a toll-gate, while the unharnessed donkey was grazing some distance ahead. "Why, William," she said, "are you going to do the donkey's work?" "No, marmt'l was the reply, with a cunning wink. "But oi bamt a-goin' to pay for 'e to go through when'e can go through by hisself without payin' an' oi can harness 'e oop afterwards."</p>
        <p>BLANCHARD'S FEMALE PILLS " NEVER FAIL." Lrz iiclmnarledge.d to be the only g,enninr,eraedy for Correcting JLARTIEStf; OBSTRUCTS vr o,rtri to cure the :mist obstinate case,and bring about zliat is desired after all else 1111 A. Thousands of Testimonials• 111 boxes. IL 1.1 d., 2s. 9d. ; stronv, 4s. 61., Fr nil Chemists or sent free from observation by post from V. 13 11-1-A.2'i'CliAltl), Forest 111, Daiston, London,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>LADIES' LETTER.</head>
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR NELLIE,</p>
        <p>I shall commence my letter this week with a subject especially dear to the feminine heart, viz., bats. What a variety of headgear we are now seeing displayed by ladies everywhere. Along every parade there is to be seen every variety of shade of hat trimmings, whilst the hats for the most part have wide brims. I think it is very wise on our part that we adopt a headgear so suitable for protecting us from the rays of the summer sun which has now and for some time past been shining in a most delightful manner. I saw a fine hat worn by a lady at a very fashion. able south coast seaside resort the other day. The hat was of light green straw with folds and bows of green gauze run with beb6 ribbon and trails and bunches of mauve and purple phlox—a delightful contrast in colour. On the left hand side the brim of the hat went up to meet a plume of feathers. The effect was very nice.</p>
        <p>In indoor dresses a large variety of summer fabrics are being shown in all the fashionable shops just now. And they are very appropriate, too. Many persons desire a dress in which they can go for a walk and which at the same time will not be too large and full to wear in the house. But a very remarkable feature of the season's fashions is the wealth of lace, chiffon and tulle trimmings. There is, too, a tendency to employ on every pos. Bible occasion muslin and other thin transparent productions. The white muslin frock is an old fashion—probably of about 25 years ago. .1. need hardly say that I do not remember them being worn, but I have been told so by friends much older than myself A very fine muslin dress was worn by a friend of mine just recently. The bodice was made so that one side overlapped the other. A stiff white linen collar was around her neck. She was also wearing a very large tie—much the same as the large silk handkerchief ties. She looked charming.</p>
        <p>Another friend of mine, who has a great reputa- tion for smartness, paid me a visit yesterday. She had been wearing an exquisite light blue straw hat. She had also a light fawn coat cut similarly to so many we now see about. Of course as she stayed and had tea with me these outer garments were taken off. It was then I discovered the fine bodice she was wearing underneath. It was of pink muslin and was pleated in such a pretty fashion that I at once thought of you. I wished you had seen it. The chief art, however, in the making was the arrangement of the pleats. There were not too many of them, and each was about half an inch wide. There were also revers, and these were lined with a soft white material. Inside the chest was displayed a charming silk front. Out of the collars showed some very pretty cream lace.</p>
        <p>What a huge variety of sunshades and parasols are now being exhibited in all the fashionable parks of London and many provincial towns? All colours flit before the eye and help to give more life and animation, if that were possible, to many interesting scenes. Smart parasols are profusely trimmed with lace to match the rest of the toilett, while silk sunshades have appliqués of fine black lace forming a border, and others have frills placed on the outside and gauged linings of chiffon inside. Coloured silk parasols are others braid through with chiffon of a contrasting shade which has a little puffing at the edge. Would you like to have a description of a pretty infant's yoke sacque. Then I will give you one. It is single to make and is very popular. Use three skeins of cream white yarn for the body and one skein of colour for the :yoke. One No. 3 bone crochet needle. Use about 5i yards ribbon. For yoke—Chain 100, three rows of double crochet, taking up back stitch to form a rib. Work tightly. Fourth row—Widen on twenty-fifth and twenty- eighth stitches, and have 44 stitches between on back of yoke. Also widen on twenty-fifth and twenty-eighth stitches from opposite front. Widen every row, having just two stitches between the wideuings, then 4,6, 8, 10, 12, 14, and up to 30. Aiways keep 44 stitches between widenings on the back, and first widenings twenty-fifth and twenty- eighth stitches from fronts. Begin jacket part with a shell of 4 treble crochet in every other stitch of yoke, until you reach the first point of yoke, then make a chain of 28 stitches and carry on to other point of yoke, with shell in every other stitch until you reach the third point of yoke, then make a chain of 29 stitches and carry it to fourth point of yoke, join with shell, and make shell in every other stitch across second front. Make a chain of 3 and turn, and put shell on shell until you reach the chain, on which you pub 7 shells, then shell on shell across back, 7 shells in second chain, and so on across second front. Make jacket 23 shells deep. Sleeve.—Join in wool under arm, and make shell on shell on jacket, and shell in every other stitch on yoke from first row, 22 shells in all. Then 18 rows shell on shell. Around bottom of jacket and sleeves a row of 5 double crochet shells. Around neck a row of holes for ribbon, finished with shells.</p>
        <p>Your loving JElanii;</p>
        <p>OLD FALSE LEELff 130UURT.</p>
        <p>Many ladies and gentlemen have by them old or die used false teeth, which might as well be turned into money. Messrs. R. D. &amp;J. H. Fraser,of Princes-street Ipswich, established since 1333, buy old false teeth. If you send your teeth to them they will remit you by return post the utmost value ; or, if preferred, they will make you the best offer, and hold the teeth over for your reply. If reference necessary, apply tolMessrs. Bacon Z.; Co., Bankers, Ipswich.—Advl.</p>
