      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899</p>
        <p>"WOMEN'S CHAT."</p>
        <p>The most tactful member of our Royal Family is un- doubtedly the Princess of Wales. Her Royal Highness always has the right word ready at the right moment, and can invariably smooth over rough places with a simple unstudied grace, not a few women would give half their kingdom to possess. The most tactless mem- ber is perhaps the Empress Frederick. Her Majesty is one of the most clever women in Europe, and can tackle any man living on scientific and political subjects, but is lamentably deficient in the quality which has helped so greatly to endear the Princess of Wales to her future subjects. Of late years the Duchess of Fife has become an in- veterate theatre goer, and when in town rarely indeed misses a first night. To the French custom of wearing high-necked, long-sleeved gowns at the Play, her Royal Highness adheres, and is never seen at this particular festivity, in the conventional evening dress. It is well known that the Duchess greatly admires the mode, but apart from this altogether it has long been deemed by her physicians a desirable precaution against chills to which the Duchess is somewhat susceptible. If all women would follow her Royal Highness's example, we should probably hear less about colds contracted in the draughty theatres. The gathering of Royalties, at the Prime Minister's garden party at Hatfield, exceeded that of several previous years. The Princess of Wales, owing to Lady Salisbury's indisposition was absent, it is true, but the Prince of Wales the Duke and Duchess of York, Prince Franbis of Teck, the Grand Duke Michael of Russia, and the Crown Prince of Siam, honoured the entertainment with their presence, and spent some time in making a tour of the gardens, and in chatting with friends. It was James I. ciho exchanged the Manor of Hatfield with his minister Robert Cecil, Marquis of Salisbury,for another manor and park. Its new master erected the present magnificent quadrangular mansion, which is one of the finest specimens of the baronial buildings of that age in the kingdom. In 1835, a portion of the house was destroyed by fire,in the flames of which the Dowager Marchioness of Salisbury perished, but it was shortly afterwards restored with great taste, and in exactly the old style. Near Hatfield is Brocket Hall, in which Lord Palmerston died in 1865.</p>
        <p>There is a strong family likeness between the children of the late Dm hess of Teek, and a handsomer pair than the Duchess of York and Prince Francis, it would in- deed be difficult to find. The Duchess of York, is, and ever has been devoted to her brothers, and takes the keenest interest in each step in their career. Prince Francis is almost a giant in stature, much darker than our future Queen' and a splendidly built handsome figure. When in full field dress with his helmet and tall black plume on, the top of the plume can not be much under seven and a halt feet from the ground. He was sducated at Wellington and Sandhurst, and has served in the 9th Lancers, and in the King's Royal Rifles. He is now a major in the tat Dragoons. Rheumatism, coughs, colds, and the divers effects of chills, we hear of every day, and little wonder. The sun shines brilliantly, and one sallies forth, arrayed in airy fairy costume, feeling secure that with May, the cold winds have at length departed: Ere long the sun retires, a wind one can scarcely ter m balmy gets up, and one has either to borrow or buy a wrap, or—face the cons' quences ef a thorough chill. A more treacherous time than we have lately passed through, few can re- member. _ _ _</p>
        <p>At a garden party, quite recently, I noticed several women had peronised the transparent yoke, and sleeves. The brilliant morning had given place to a dull, gloomy, chilly afternoon, and I could but marvel how long these foolish individuals would escape some of the ills flesh is heir to. In this country it is nothing less than madness to venture out of doors m gowns of this description. When the sun beats fiercely down, brown-red necks and arms, are inevitable, and when the wind blows and the sun disappears, pneumonia is the usual result. The macintosh of to-day is quite an object of grace. Jaunty little coats are made of that white rubber which has long been left to the glorification of the coachman. They have their collars faced with gay scarlet or blue silk, and pipings of the same appear at appropriate junctures. Another variety has a shapely yoke with simulated stitchings and loose bishop sleeves. To a cic yclist such a protection is a necessity. These little coats roll up very neatly on the handle bars, and serve to keep one dry during the sharpest shower. The present cycling season has been, unhappily, characterised by an exceptional number of serious acci- dents, to women riders. Usually these are not produced so much by lack of skill, as by the rider's attention being momentarily divert A, amid crowded traffic, from the exegencies of steering. A fertile cause of disaster is the practice of taking dogs for an airing. These faithful attendants usually lag behind after a few miles, and the owners have to constantly leok back to see if they are following. That is always dangerous on wheel hack, unless the eyclist is'exceptionally expert, being provoca- tive of both collision and capsize. A bicycle, just completed in Vienna, has. cent no less than £5,500 to build. The wheel of exquisite beauty is inlaid ith precious stones in every possible part, and the machine is about to be aespatched to the wife of a South African millionaire. One often bears of the bicycle thief lying in wait on country roads, for the unsuspect- ing, unprotected female, but with a prize such as this, the South African millionairess willsurely need an escort of strongly armed men. Bathing costumes are now occupying the minds of fashionable modistes, and they are paying as much at- tention to this aquatic costume as a few weeks ago they were to race frocks,and ball toilettes. A delightful cos- tume I was shown at a leading modistes the other day, was in blue serge. The large sailor collar was of white serge, and the overhanging blouse fastened with little round ivory buttons each side on to a rather wide plastron of white serge, and there was a like band on the short sleeves, and on the knickerbockers. There was no skirt to this cesturne—one is much less hampered without one. If there is a walk of any distance going into, and coming out of the sea,a bathing dressing gown is most convenient. The nicest I have seen this year was of a soft white woolly material. You simply wrap it round you. Some women like to be as dressed as possible and some as undressed as possible for the sea, but the essential part te be considered is combined decency and comfort. _ _ _</p>
        <p>A word of caution about bathing, which is, in itself, exercise. A bath ought never to be taken when one is overheated, as it is then more or less dangereus. To take a bath when exhausted too is unwise, as there is little probability of thorough reaction, and vitality will be lowered, as a coosequence. Neither should a bath be taken just before eating, or for two hours after. Wben a bath is taken, the blood and vitality are in the skin. The call is there. If the stomach be filled with food at the same time.theblood and vitality cannot carry on two such vital offices well, and the secretion of gastric juice will be seriously interfered with. Good judgment must be used in such matters.</p>
