      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>WHAT THINK YE OF CHRIST? Air. C. Roberts, of Warringtm, in lecturing on the above subject in the Christadelphian Room, Bold street, 013. Sunday, examined the position which Christ occupied according to Scripture tEstimony, and contrasted it with the spurious Christ, which the Churches profesesi to believe in. The Lecturer, in comparing the earliest Prophetic references in the Old Testament, and the last prophetic utterance in the New respecting Christ in the light of the life which Christ lived, and the claims Which he made when on earth, with the Christ taught in the Churches ; was most anxious that his hearers should arrive at correct Scriptural conclusions, as wrong ideas en this subject were of vital moment. Mr. Roberts, in BPeaking of the twofold character of Christ, as son of tnan,and son of God directed attention to the prophecy foretelling the overthrow of theithrone of David,(Ezikiel 21.27) which was fulfilled in doe course, and the angels promise to Mary (Luke i. 32) that the throne of David should be given to Christ, which was yet to be fulfilled. Christ was not as some stated, sitting on David's throne in heaven now, for as a matter Gif plain scripture statement " David is not ascended into the heavens" (Luke ii, 34) aud Christ is sat down on the right hand of God tor a definite pericd, ie until his enemies are made his footstool. When this was finished Christ would return from heaven, as the angels told the illen of Galilee he wool I do (Luke i. x;) and there were InhanT events tran,pired, and transpiring, which led n joyfully to anticipate that there was only a short "Me to elapse before Christ would be in the earth again, arid in these evil days, when God's word was ignored Christ's claims ridiculed, they were invited by a pelief in the Gospel and obedience on baptism, to enter Into the way which if followed faithfully would entitle them to a participation in the glories of the coining age.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>WARRINGTON CORPORA- c TION BILL IN PARLIAMENT. I</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON CORPORA- c TION BILL IN PARLIAMENT. I</p>
        <p>RESUMED PROCEEDINGS. THE SHIP CANAL COMPANY'S OPPOSITION.</p>
        <p>WESTMINSTER, TUESDAY.</p>
        <p>To-day, the Police and Sanitary Committee of the House of Commons resumed the consideration of this Bill. Sir Stafford Northcote presided. When the Com- mittee adjourned last Friday tte case for the Manchester Ship Cdll%l with reference to the Water Works part of Bill was being presented, and evidence was being given by Mr. Hunter, engineer to the Ship Canal Company. Thl counsel for the promoters were Mr. Pembroke Stephens, Q C., Mr. Wedderburn and Mr. Ram ; for Sir Gilbert Greenall, Mr. Balfour Browne, QC., and Mr. E. H. Lloyd; and for the Ship Canal Company, Mr.Pember, Q.C., Mr. Balfour Browne, Q.C., and Mr. Du Cane. As soon as the Committee met the room was cleared for consultation, and for half-an-hour parties waited outside. When they were admitted, Mr. PEMBROKE STEPHENS said: lam told that the Manchester Ship Canal Company are asking as compen- sation a great deal more than we should ever be able to impound. I want to make that quite clear. Mr. Du CANE: Surely, Mr. Stephens, you have closed your case. Mr. STEPHENS : I don't wish the Committee to be un- der any misapprehension-- The CHAIRMAN: Your case is closed. Mr. STEPHENS said that he would be bound, if:the Canal Company asked for so much compensation water, to ask the Committee to allow him to recall his engineer to make it quite clear that it could not be given. If the Committee decided on the point before them on the as- sumption that the figures given by Mr. Hunter were correct, then with great respect their decision would be wrong; Mr. DTI CANE: If our witnesses make a !mistake it will beopen to my learnel friend to cross-examine them. _ .</p>
        <p>Sir E. LEADER WILLIAMS, who designed the Manches- ter Ship Canal and saw it carried out, said that when the tides did not rise high enough at Eastham they de- pended on the land water for the section between Latch- ford and Eastham. The water of Grange Mill and Water Mill brooks was included in their calculations, and it was most necessary that they should continue to get that water. He had no doubt about the streams sending water into the canal. While the canal was being constructed wooden troughs had to be constructed to carry the water over the works so as to prevent the flooding of the works. He did not suppose the Warring- ton Corporation would be there that day asking for more reservoir accommodation if there was no water coming down the streams. He agreed with Mr. Hunter's evidence as to the amount of water. It was at the in- stance of the Corporation of Warrington that the Canal Company were restricted as to the amount of Mersey water that was to go into the canal and were bound to send the Mersey and Weaver water into the tidal waters or the estuary, and now the Corporation were trying to take away the little they had left. The Corporation were also pumping enormous quantities from the Mersey themselves to supply the town. Money compensation was of no use to the Ship Canal. They must have water. _ _ _</p>
        <p>By Mr. J. W. WILSON (one of the committee) : The Appleton reservoir was made before 1885. Sir LEADER WILLIAMS, further examined, said he ob- jected to the increased pressure in the proposed new pipe through the culvert. It would throw a greater liability upon the Canal Company than was done by the Act of 1885. The culvert was designed to resist pres- sure from above and not from below, and if this pipe with a high pressure was put in there would be a greater risk if it burst.</p>
        <p>Cross-examined by Mr. STEPHENS : If he said the Corporation were pumping Mersey water he made a mistake, he meant the traders of Warrington. but prac- tically it came to the same thing. It might be that these traders had statutory powers. He did not know the capacity of the existing Appleton reservoir nor of the proposed Dennow reservoir. He had not gone into the question of capacity, he spoke on the main principle, which was the amount of water which came from these brooks for locking purposes. But what you do with water miles away has nothing to do with the quantity of water you at present get from Appleton reservoir. All you are interested in, according to your own petition, is the overflow from Appleton reservoir. Can you tell me how much it is ? Sir L. WILLIAMS : Clearly all we want:is the status quo. Of course we will still get the water that goes into the stream in the mile and a half below the Appleton reservoir. _ _ . _</p>
        <p>Mr. STEPHENS : Therefore the whole question between us is Dennow reservoir and its capacity in addi- tion to the c ipacity of the existing works. Mr. DtPKENNA (one of the Committee) : You have given, Sir Leader, no evidence at all of the quantity you get now. Nor did Mr. Hunter give us the faintest evidence. All he said wag that they obtained 550.000 gallons per day on an average. That does not in the least assi6t us as to how much ynu get in dry weather. rerbaps you get water only on 200 days out cf the 365. Mr. STEPHENS : And that average is one and a half miles below Appleton reservoir. Sir LEADER WILLIAMS: I have relied, of course, upon Mr. Hunter's figures. We don't want to rob the Cor- poration of anything; Supposing only 50 gallons a day cense down, we are asking for that quantity. Cross-examined as to the high-pressure pipe in the culvert, Sir LEADER WILLIAMS said that if there was no burst there would be no damage.butif there was a buret there would be some risk to the upper portion of the culvert being lifted, causing very heavy expense to the Canal Company to repair. There had been a burst, and no damage was done to the culvert, but then the differential "head" was in favour of the culvert. But with the high pressure pipe the "head" would be against the culvert. He did not know whether or not the Cinal Company had put any pipes through the culverts. Mr. STEPHENS : Then you may take it from me that the Canal Company have lifted a hydrauliepipe which formerly went clear of the culvert, and put it into the culvert. Supposing that there is a pressure of 7001 b or 800113 on that pipe, what would happen to the culvert if it buret ? Sir LEADER WILLIAMS: There would not be enough water to half fill the culvert. Mr. BALFOUR BROWNE did not seek to re-examine this witness, and said that closed his evidence. 't Mr. M'Kenns. asked that Mr. Hunter should here- called, but before this was done the committee room was again cleared. MR. GOUGH TELEGRAPHED FOR.</p>
        <p>On parties being re-admitted, The CHAIRMAN, addressing Mr. Stephens, said :—I nave received several communications by to-day's post from Warrington. Most of then, of course, the Com- mittees will take no notice of, as they are anonymous. But there is one gentleman who writes—Mr. Gough— who says he is hon. secretary of the Warringtnn Rate- payers and Proprrty Owners' Association ; and he en- closes certain papers alleging improper practices in con- nection with the taking of the poll for the promotion of this Rill. After full consideration we think that probably the best thing will be that we should have this gentleman up and see whether he is in a position to Prove the allegations which he sends to the Committee. Therefore, I propose to instruct the Clerk to the Com- mittee to wire to him and give us the opportunity of cross-examining him. Mr. STEPHENS : If he if., the gentleman who has issued this document (holding up a Paper) from the Prop3rty Owners and Ratepapers' Association, I shall be very glad to see him. (Laughter.) The CLERK was then instructed to telegraph to Mr. Gough requiring his attendance. Mr. HUNTER, chief engineer to the Canal Company. recalled, said in reply to Mr. M`Kenna that he asked for 550,000 gallons a day for four days a fortnight. The average he gave to the Committee was not taken on days of excessive rainfall, and the minimum supply would be 33 per cent. less. The average minimum might be put at 450,000 gallons a day. NPKENNA. : Surely the important point to you is that the minimum shall be guaranteed ? WITNESS : No, it might happen that an exceptionally low tide and excessively dry weather might be concur- rent. What I want is that I should have the quantity of water, speaking generally, that I have now on those days in each fortnight in which the tide does not go into tbe canal. In reply to a question by Mr. Bill (a membn of the Committee), Mr. HUNTER. said he could not say what proportion of the quantity he had mentioxed came from tbe Appleton renereoir and what came from the brooks below. He bad gauged the water where it entered the canal, and could not differentiate between the two. COMPENSATION WATER FOR THE SHIP CANAL</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>COMPANY.</head>
        <p>COMPANY.</p>
        <p>Mr. BLLFOUR BROWN—E,-Q.C.. then addressed the Com- mittee on behalf of the Canal Company, urging that thit question of the water qupply was of very consider- able importanco to that great undertaking, e3penially as their powsrs with regard to the great rivers were strictly limited. No necessity had been proved which would warrant the Committee to deprive the Canal Company of that water, merely to supply Warrington' trade pur- poaem. The scheme took not only the water (If till Urange Mill and Water Mill works hut front the D tra- bury valley. Parliament in such cases always required compensation water to he given. At this point the learned caunsel was interrupted by his junior, with the result that Mr. BROWSE Raid : lam told I need ect an further on the question of enmpeniation water. The Corporation have conceded what I was No ably arguing for. (Laugh- ter.) Mr. WEDDERBURN, Q.C.. for the promoters : NVe have agre7d on our figure on 62,000 gallons a gay coming from the Appleton reservior. Mr. APKENNA : And what have you given ?</p>
        <p>M. WEDDERBURN : Thor will get on our figures on four days each fortnight 217,000 gallons a day rneliured at our Appleton reservoir. Mr. BROWSE then turned to tha question of the high</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899.</p>
        <p>pressure pipe passing through the culvert, and argued that the Corporation now sought to make such an alter- ation of the conditions under which the Canal Company constructed the culvert that the Corporation and not the Canal Company should be made liable for any damage or risk.</p>
        <p>Mr. M'KENNA said It was quite clear that when the Canal Cmpany got their Bill and undertook tl pay for the construction of the culvert the Corporation could duplicate, enlarge, and alter their pipes passing through it. Therefore not only existing but future works were provided for. Mr. WILSON . But is it not quite clear under that agreement no reservation was made as to the pressure on the mains ?</p>
        <p>_ Mr BROWNE : I think it must have teen quite well understood that the prtssure then existing would con- tinue.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. W. WILSON. But how can you limit the pres- sure where the water is pumped? Mr. BROWNE : The water wai pumped into the racier- voir and then was supplied to the town by gravita- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. M`Kerma : Had there been a rising main at the time the Canal Company made their culvert von would have been placed under the same obligations? Mr. BROWNE : Possibly we should have, but then we should have made the culvert differently. Mr. M'KENNA : You were put under these conditions because the inhabitants of Warrington were there before you. Mr. BROWNE : And now I say they should be put under conditions seeing that we are there and they are proposing to alter the present state of things. Mr. ACKENNA : It would be cheaper to have a heavier and stronger pipe than to alter the culvert. Why don't you ask that the pipe should be one to your satisfac- tion?</p>
        <p>Mr. BROWNE : I think they should take the responsi- bility. Mr. STEPHENSI replied, contending that the legal guarantee the Canal Company had lay in the fact that a burst pipe would be an enormous loss to the Carp3ra- tion.</p>
        <p>Mr. 11. J. WILSON (who had taken the chair during the absence of Sir S. Northcote) asked Mr. Stephens if the Corporation would have had the right to make and lay this high pressure pipe without coming to Parlia- ment had the culvert not been there.</p>
        <p>M. STEPHENS replied that he thought they would. It was not necessary to come to Parliament for wffat would have simply been an improvement of the existing condition of things.</p>
        <p>After the Committee had again deliberated in private</p>
        <p>The CHammaN said : We have decided to ask the Canal Cmnpany to bring up a clause for their own pro- tection proposing any specifications, tests, or other guarantees they think necessary as to this pips. It was agreed that the clause should be brought up on Wednesday at eleven o'clock, and the C)maaittee then adjourned.</p>
        <p>You employed some canvassers ?—No. Do yo d say tbat n)body went round to put your views before the ratepayers ?—rfne members of the Association did so themselves.</p>
        <p>Then why should the canvassers of the Corporation not do so i—Tney not only put their views but they filled up the papers. _ _ . .</p>
        <p>Can you identify or produce a sieg,le paper with regard to which that statement is true ? —lf they will snow me the Cockhedze-lane papers I will. Can you produci any person who says his payer was filled in without his knowledge ?—Yes. Is lir, here?—No; but I can give his name and address.</p>
        <p>lam told that that statement is totalby untrue. you knew, as a matter of fact that a large numbr of voting papers against the Bill were all in the same hand- writing ?—That may be so. Are Mr. Holbrolk anl Mr. Clavton.the political agents whom we employei, the men whom you say did these improper things ?—No. Who do you say behaved improperly 9—Andrew Dillon for cne.</p>
        <p>Are you aware that if you had these serious allegations to make you could have proved them before the Local Government Board ?—I did not know I could have done that. _ _</p>
        <p>You could have appeared as a ra.tepavers association against the Bill ? Yes, but we hadn't the money. You personally have been once or twice in difficulties with the Corporation I)—What do you mean ? On twe occasions you.who are a furnitare dealer.have been summoned and convicted—l would rather say sue- :%.essfullv summoned for keeping your gools on the streets ?—Yea I have been summoned.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. J. WIL3ON (to witness) : Whem the policemen went round did anybody go with them ?—Yes, two men in the employ of the Corporation. Mr. WEDDERBURN : Is it a fact Mr. Gough, that you said you wou'd withdraw your demand for a poll if you were allJweJ to expose your goxis on tae footpath? No.</p>
        <p>Did you not say so to Mr. Peacock ?—No. Mr. LEurr (one of the committee) : Was it by some spscial arrangement that one of each party should accompany each policeman? Mr. IWEDDERBURN : There was nn such arrangement. Mr. H.J. WILSON : I don't understand why anybody accon:pamied the policemen. Mr. GounH : We did make application to accompany when we knew that the Corporation were employing men to go round, but the Town Clark rgused to allow us. _ _ _</p>
        <p>Mr BOYCE (of the Local Government Board) said he did r3,t think Mr. Gou,gh's Association ha l any right to be present at the counting of the votes. Mr. WHITTLE Town Clerk of Warrington, in reply to Mr. Welderburn, said that when the poll was demanded he was instructed to do what had ten done before and he apvinted the two politioal agents to conduct the poll as there was no department of th 3 Corporation with a large enough staff for that purpose. He saw a large number of the voting papers when they were counted. Is it true to say Witt on a large number of the pipers in favour of the Bill the signatures were in the same handwriting? I distinctly deny that. Was there any canvassing for the Corporation P Mr. Holbrook will answer that. Mr. LEIITY . What were the men paid? I don'z know. The account; was passed by the Finance Com- mittee and I did not see it. Mr. GOUGEI : May I ask the Town Clerk a question ?</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : Through me you may. Mr. Gronoa : Will you ask the Town Clerk if he said in his office that if his committee gave him orders that they should employ money on purpose to win this 'ere poll? The CHAIRMAN : That is a very serious allegation ycu wish me to put to the Town Clerk. Mr. WHITTLE : I wish to deny it most emphatically. Mr. Gough said that he applied for leave f it- persons to accompany the po:ice and that I rAused it. That is perfectly true and I gave deliberate instructions to our agents that no persons were to go with the police. Mr. WILLIAM Hourao )11, political agent for the Con- servative party at Warrington, said he was instructed with Mr. John Clayton, the Liberal agent, to take the poll in the same way as the old election or: guardians. At first he did not employ any canvassers but when he found out that Mr. Gough was working against the Bill and making headway, he mentioned the matter to a member of the Corporation, and then men were put on to distribute the Mayor's letter explaining the Bill. He gave the men instructions to go to every house where a policeman left a voting paper, not with the policeman, bet after him and to solicit vot?s on behalf of toe Cor- poration. No one was to fill up a voting paper unless tie voter was piesent and requirei assistance. Were any instructions given to fill up votinz papers for parsons against their will ?—Oa the contrary I told them they must not do that. Tio6 CisatrotaN : How were These canvassers paid?</p>
        <p>WITNESS: Tney werJ pid 7s. 61,per day and 2a. 61. or each evening. _</p>
        <p>Is it true that they were to gdt an arlditional nvment if they sneceeaed in getting votes in iaviur of to ? —No.</p>
        <p>Mr. H. J. WILSON : Is 10s. a day a usual payment for such work?</p>
        <p>WITNESS : Yes for caul work of that kind. It is the only kind of work for which we ctn pay them. Mr. LEUTY : Did you not coeceiye that your position as an independent party taking a poll of the ratepayers precluded you from seeking to influeece the poll? WITNESS: No. But the opponents were Canvassing. Toe CHAIRMAN : Tney were not taking the poll. paid these expenses? WITNESS : The Corporation. The CHM FM nr, ratepayers money svls taken to pay for the printing and circu'ation of the Mayor's let- ter and for the canvaising of toe Bill. I should like to know if that is allowed by the auditor of the Lo:al Gevernment Board. _</p>
        <p>Mr. BOYCE : We have nothing te do with the audit of a Cerperatien's accounts. Mr. H. J. WitsoN : Are the aulitere ot the borough of Warrington professional accountants ? Mr. WEDDERBURN : There are two eleeted auditors, and both are professional aceountants. The Cnamean (to witness) : What wig the total ex- nesse of the po!I £10). Mr. JOHN CLAYTON, Lib3ral agent, eaid he was present when Mr. Holbrook gave instructions to the canvassers, and agreed wit'3 him that they were toll not to go with the police. He understood that they kept a few doors behind the police, and he himself saw some about half a street behind the officers. The CHAIRMAN here askel Mr. Gough whether he I.”lieved the canvasiers were in any house at the same time as the police, and his reply was "Oh yes ; I know they were; I sim them. And when the policemin did not collect the papers, the canvasser went into the house filled up the paper, if it wasn'c filled up, without the consent of the p Arty, and then took i: to the police." How many of such cases did you see? I have eigh- teen cases down here (pointing to a h3llc). They nor. only went with papers after the policerwn had got two or thre3 doors away, but were collecting up to E.ix o'clock, althoweil the police ceasei at 4. Mr. H. J. WILSON : Did o:her members of your As- sociation see the same things done ? Ys I can mention parties who are willing to give evidence if required. The Committee then deliberated in private for a long time, and on the re-admission of the public they askrd Mr. Wedderburn if he had any further evidence to Call. He replied he had only some with regar.4 to the nature of the Association. The Committee said they did not want that.</p>
        <p>_ Mr. WYDDERBUR!,7 addressing the committee said that undoubtedly serious allegations had been made by Mr. Gough, but they had been made without that notice to the Corporation which would have enab?ed them to filly deal with them. The allegations were, he submitted,not distinct and definite enough to weigh with the commit- tee against the Bill. They had not beea definitely told what undue influence had been exerted and even if it were true that in 18 cases the papers had been filled up by the canvassers he would sae,gest to the committee that that did not au i could not invalidate the over- whelming vote in favour of the Bill. He would suggest that the Corporation were not parties to the canvassing and th it the political agents, t o the manner bred, could not resist it and without consulting the Corporation re- sorted to that method, whose use he regrettei. Mr. LEIITY : Ds you sugg-st it was without the knowledge of the Corporation ? _ -</p>
        <p>Mr. WEDDERBURN said he was so informed. Mr. H. J. WILSON : They paid, for it. Mr. WEDDERBURN : They ultimately did EQ. Mr. LEUTY : Without any protest from you. Mr. WEDDERBURN admitted that was the case. He however urged the committee not to kive to the vague allegations made by Mr. Gough anythine: like the earzw importance as if they had been allegations which had been most carefully and fully gone into. So long as they were satisfied that the mind of the people of Warrintton had been well ascertained the purpose of the committee would have been fulfilled. If it were necessary the Cor- poration would be ready to adjourn the consideration of the Bill until another poll could be taken. The CHAIRMAN here asked Mr Gough if he had offered to any of the petitioning :opponents of the Bill to give evidence for them. Mr. Gouan : No I have not. _</p>
        <p>The CHLIRM&amp;N : Perhaps I may say that what is most present in he minds of the ceeemittee is not so much Mr. Goug,h's evidence as the question of the Cor- poration having empliyeti ratepayeremoney to influence the result of the poll. You see you were fighting Mr. Gough and his friends with their own money as rate- payers.</p>
        <p>Mr. WEDDERBURN said that so long as the Committee were satisfied that the Corporatim had got the sense of the people behind them the question of whether the poll was properly or improperly obtained was a minor affair.</p>
        <p>Mr. Gogort : The allegations I have made in respect to the practices during the poll have b :en admitted by the Cerporation and their servants. In rPtOv to the Chairman, Mr. Wedderburn said that over 11,000 voting papers were issued and that the num- ber actually polled was 9,656. The Committee again cleared the roma for private de- liberation and on parties being readmitted. A REPROOF FOR THE CORPORATION.</p>
        <p>The Cr:anima:sr said • The Committee must place on record their disapproval of the use of the ratepayers' money and the employment of pplitical agents practic- ally for the purpose of influencing the e,ectors as ad- mitted by the promoters' evidence but at the same time they feel that were they to reject the modified Bill the ratepayers would still have tu pay the expenses cf the promotion of an unsuccessful Bill, the provisions of the Borough Funds Act having been complied with. They are therefore not prepared to go the length of rejecting the Bill or of requiring a fresh poll to be taken but they think it their duty to place this ex pressipa of opinion on record.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>TA.KING OF THE POLL. SERIOUS ALLEGATIONS. MR. S. GOUGH EXAMINED.</head>
        <p>THE TA.KING OF THE POLL. SERIOUS ALLEGATIONS. MR. S. GOUGH EXAMINED.</p>
        <p>WESTMINS'PER, WEDNESDAY.</p>
        <p>The Police and Sanitary Committee to-day further considered this Bill. Sir Stafford Northcote presided. It was arranged to take till opposition of tne Incorpor- ated Law Society to Clause 153, which was as fo:lows : "The surveyor or inspector of nuisances and any °Meer authorised by name specially or generally in that behalf by the Corporation may lay information or complaints or appear institute and carry on any proceedings under this Act before a court of summary jurisdiction." Mr. Hollams appeared for the incorporated Law Society. Mr. WEDDERBURN, Q.C., called Mr. WhitVe, rOWII Clerk of Warrington, who stated that the clause was copied from the East Ham Act of last year and that it was necessary to put the officers of the corporation in the same position under this Bill, as they were under the Public Health Act. . . .. .</p>
        <p>_ Cross-examined, Mr. Wrttrrrx said he did not think it sufficient to give an official power to lay informations or complaints. It was thought advisable that he should also be able to appear in court to prosecute.—Such an officer would be an "unqualified person" under the Solicitors Act ?—He would.—And for the last fifty years the statute law has prohibited the appearance in courts of justice of unqualified persons P—Our officers can now nonappear under the Health Act. The Eastham Clause slipped through without attract- ing the vigilant eye of the Incorporated Law Society. Mr. WEDDERBURN : But it was discussed by this Committee and passed. Mr. HOLL&amp;MS (to witness) : Would you not take the clause in the Edmonton Act by which your officers would have power to lay information and clmplaints ? WITNESS: No. I would be quite prepared to substi- tute section 259 of the Public Health Act. Mr. HOLLVIS : Any officer, however humble, should you think possess powers to attend and prosecute ? WITNESS: Any officer autlaorisel by the Corporation. Mr. H. J. WILSON (one of the Committee) : Can you give me an instance under this Bill? WITNESS : Prosecutions with regard to common lodging houses. -</p>
        <p>Who -would prosecute there ?—The Insp.tctor of Nuis- SEIM.</p>
        <p>Mr. HOLLOIS azreed that the clause should be re- stricted to the Edmonton precedent under whicn no power was given to these officers to appear in court to prosecute. The Committee decided that the E imonton prnzelent must be followed. MR. GOUGH'S EVIDENCE.</p>
        <p>Mr. SANEUEL GOUGE. Secretary to the Warrinzton Property Owners and Ratepayers Asiocivion, called by the committee in consequence of his letter to the Chair- man, said that his firat complaint was that the provi- sions of the bill were Rot sufficiently advertised to bring them to the knowledge of the ratepayers. Hi 4 next com- plaint was that only a few aldermen and councillors were at the first public meeting, and some of these spoke complainingly about the large expenditure that was pro- posed. He was going on to speak of the capacity of Winwick Co supply all the water Warrington would re- quire for many years, and to argue that the Corporation should first pr meed with street improvements when The CILLIIIIILN said that what the witness was called to speak of was the promotion of the Bill, and the allega- tion of improper practices in taking the poll. The wit- ness had seat him a letter which appeared in a Warring- ton paper on March 18th, from his association. Was it a fact, as was therein alleged, that the Town Clerk re- fused to allow a representative of the Association to be present at the counting of the votes ? WITNESS : It is.</p>
        <p>You further say that arrangements were made with the agents and the canvassers by which the latter were to have 7s. 63. per day and a furthQr 2s. 61. a day if they got a majority in favour of the Bill. Is that a true statement ?—lt is.</p>
        <p>You say further that clerks and canvassers were em- ployed to colect the voting papers after the police had finished their collection and atter the poll had elosed.—l was told so. And you further say that the Slcretary of the Associa- tion, judging by what he saw, thought that 90 p:a. cent. of the voting papers were filled up by the canvassers, being in one handwriting ?—Yes, that is so. Is there anything more you wish to say upon this specific poiat of the means wh,reby this Bill was adopted by the ratepayers ?—lf this poll hai been t Ikea under the Ballot Act I i'na quite confident that the Cor- poration would not have got a majority tor the Bill. You know that the committee have struck out the Daresbury portion of the water proposals. Would that fact remove your objection to the Bill ?-oh, no. Do you say of your own knowledge that voting papers were filled in without the consent of the voters ? —Yes, nn my own knowledge I say that I saw them. Mr. H. J. Wir.sos (one of the committee) : That is a most seriom thing to say. Mr. WEDDERBUFIII, Q.C., before cross-examining, said he would call the people responsible for conducting the poll. The WITNESS in cross-eratnination said the fact that the Daresbur Y part had been struck out with an expendi- ture of £113,000 did alter his views to som,laxtent but if the promoters intended to go on with the Hill Warren reservoir he still objec od to the Bill. The present Rate- payers ksiociation was re-organised at the beginning of the year. On the 18th January witness attended the ratepayers meeting and demanded the poll. It was after that that the ratepayers Association was reformed. It had now SO members, at the time of the poll there were 40. He was not the only active member. The executive of the Association issued a printed document about the Bill. He did not attend the subsequent ratepayers' meeting because he had been told by a councillor that he would not be allowed to speak but could only put questions. _ .</p>
        <p>Did you claim to be present at the counting of the vot-s F—Yes.</p>
        <p>Were you informed that although you had no legal right you could bs present as a matter of courtesy ? No, I was told by the Mavor that I could only be present when the questionable votes wens examined. There was a majority of 7,000 for the Bill P—Havihg employed from 80 to 100 men you may well get this majority._</p>
        <p>Do you know that all that was done by wrions we employed was that they filled op the names and addresses ot the voters and the number of votes P—No, the agents went round with the policemen and filled up the papers.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>THE MOST NUTRITIOUS.</p>
