      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>SHIP CA.NAL COMPENSATION CLAUSE.</head>
        <p>THE SHIP CA.NAL COMPENSATION CLAUSE.</p>
        <p>A very long clause was then brought up Mr. du Cane for the Ship Canal Company to carry out the decisions of the Commatee on their petition. It was intimated that only a few points remained outstanding and these were tak,u seriatium.</p>
        <p>On the motion of the Corporation the paragraph relat- ing to the supply of compensation water was amended by the insertion of the words "So soon as the Dennow reservoir is completed." fixing that as the beginning of the operation of the clause. The quantity was put at 217,600 gallons a day for the four days every fortnight on wnich the supply is to be given. The Canal Com- pany proposed that the penalty for failure to supply should be more than £5O a day (offered by the Corpora- tion) and that for wilfully withholding they should have the additional right to bring an action for damages. This latter power the Corporation opposed as most unreasonable and Mr. M'Kenna pointed out that it would be to the interest of the Corporation to surply Winwick water to the Canal rather than pay £59 a day. The Committee kept in the penalty of £5O a day but struck out the right to bring an action. A discussion took place as to the size and strength of the high pressure pipe through the culvert. Too Canal Company wished to limit its diameter to 3 inches to ißsert specific details as to thienatureof the pipe and the way in which it was laid, and to r 'quire it to be tested up to a hydraulic pressure of 5001bs. to the square inch. The Corporation objected to the limitation in size, offered to make the pipe of steel to the design and satis- faction of the Canal Company's engineer, and agreed to the pressure test. ' Ttoe Committee adopted the Canal Company's proposa with the exception of specific details, accepting the Cor- poration's words by which the plpe shall be of steel and made to the design and satisfaction of the Canal Com- pany's engineer. The Corporation asked that words should be inserted that the pipe should be laid " without prejudice to the right of the Corporation to lay pipes and mains through the culverts," but the Committee did not think they were necessary, and they also refused the application of the Corporation that the pipe might be laid through " any of the culverts "instead of " the culvert."</p>
        <p>A numb3r of the clauses in the first part of the Bill were then agree I to, with some verhal amendments. The Committee adjourned until 12 to-morrow (Thurs- day).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>"DEADLY LEAD."</head>
        <p>THE "DEADLY LEAD."</p>
        <p>NOT THE ONLY DANGER. A TALK IN THE POTTERIES.</p>
        <p>BY Parliament, public platforms, and the press, wide- spread attention has been called to the distressing re- sults affecting the operatives from the use of lead in glazes for china aril earthenware. Indeed. S 3 much has been written and said, that a stranger visiting the Potteries and meeting a pale and palpably weak girl or woman might naturally assume that the delicate person in question was a "victim" of the insidious disease. But the odds may be that the stranger would be mistaken. Taere are treacherous causes of ill-health other than the dread.!d " lead," says the " Staffordshire Sentinel," one of the most influential newspapers in the Pot- teries, which prints the story of a local virl. This young lady—lliss Mary Underhill, of 26, Peel-street, Dresden, Staffordshire—had lived up to eight months ago, in an agricultural district, miles away from a china or earthenware manufactory, a.n3, as she had n3t enterel a pottery, she had made no acquaintance with the lead. Still, Miss Underhill was, up to a short time ago, a victim to an ailment as terri- ble and as fearful in its effects.</p>
        <p>" About two years ago," said Miss Underhill, " I was apprenticed to the dressmaking, but my energy seemed to leave me, and I could take no interest in my work. I had headaches, from which I was many times pros- trated ; my appetite was very fickle, sometimes allow- ing me to take my food and other times not ; and to aggravate my case my weakness was so bad that I was frequently seized with fainting fits. My co:our left me, and I went about as best I could, looking more like a marble figure or a waxen mole( than a living person. Mother took me to sevo,rat doctors , who said that ancemia was the cause of all my trouble. Thb doctors had very poor hopes of me. The neighbours said they had never seen a girl look worse It I went upstairs I had to sit down before I could dress inyfelf. I only went into the street once, and then I felt down in a faint. My appetite got worse. and if I did eat, I had acute indigestion, and all the time I was racked with neuralgia." Miss Underhill is now a healthy-looking girl, of win- some appearance, and a brisk assistant to her mother. "How cams you ti obtain such a remarkable revolu- a in your health ?" was asked. " Well," said Miss Underhill, as her eyes lighted up with the pleasure afforded by the recital of her story, "one day my mother read lathe "Staffordshire Soatinel" of the wonderful results achieved by Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pate People. We had also heard our friends talk of them ; they had bson recommended to us ; a friend said they had done her dau.ghter much good. I' had been so discouraged, I did not like to see any more doctors, and one day I sent fur a box of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills, and to be quite sure of getting the genuine ones sent direct to Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, London. When I had taken half of the second hoxful my colour began to come back, my appetite became better, neuralgia, giddiness, and headache ha i disap- peared, my strength began to return, and my spirits were much brighter." "And have vou kept on ?" "Oh. yes; I had six boxes. I often say to mother that it feels like being in heavers. becau,e I have been</p>
        <p>like a different person since I took them. People who have seen me before and have seen me since,cin scarcely laelieve lam the same parson. I can now help in the house and take an interest in it; indeed, I am so well, I think of going back to my dressmaking and have got a new machine for that purpose. This is, of course, only one c age among many thou- gala&amp; which Dr. Williams' Pink Pills have cured. They include cases of paralysis, locomotor ataxy. rheumatism, and sciatic t ; also all diseases arising from impoverishment of the blood, scrofula, rickets, chronic erysipelas,consumption of the bowels and lungs,anyeinia, pale and sallow complexion,general muscular weakness, loss of appetite, palpitation, paini in the back, 'nervous headache and neuralgia, early decay,all forms of female weakness, and hysteria. These pills are a tonic, not a purgative. They are genuine only with the full name, Dr. Wiiliams' pink pills for pale people. and are sold by chemists, and tot, Dr. Williams' Medicine Company, Hol- born-viaduct, London, E.C., at two and niaepence a box, or six boxes for thirt,?f,n and nine. Pills sold loose or from glasi jars are not Dr. Williams' pink pills. And substitutes never cured anyone !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON OCCASIONAL COURT.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Occasional Court, on .fonday morning, before Ar. Tiusley, John Hunt and John O'Donnell, labourers, were charged with having, some time between the 9th and 10th Jnne, at New- ton-by-Daresbury, stolen two fowl, the property of Henry Houghton. far mer.—Mr. Hough ton said that after certain information he examined hi s stack yard, and found a quantity or feathers about the place. He traced tnem to a place a short distance away where a fire had been made, and where, to all appearances, the fowl had been cooked. His wife afterwards counted the fowl on the farm and found that two were missing. The feathers on,the ground were similar to those of the missing birds. He gave information to the police.—A pDlice cmstable said he apprehended the prisoners and charged them with the theft. Hunt said at first that he was guilty, but afterwards said that he knew nothing about it. O'Donnell said they were drnnk. On being charged again that morning Hunt said he was drunk, and O'Donnell said Hunt wanted to blame it on him.—Prisoners were remanded to the Daresbury Sea one</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>WEST RALIA WINS.</head>
        <p>WEST RALIA WINS.</p>
        <p>The recent sensational advances which have taken place in the values of some of the companies working in the centre of the Kalgoarlia, field is directing attention to some of the less well-known but promising prop,rties in the same district, such as Hannan's Belle View,which has an issued capital of £82,000 in £1 fully paid shares at present quo'ed at over 25 per cent discount. Seven companies working in the same vicinity are, at present market prices, value] at over eight millions sterling, whilst Hannan' s Belle View, it should be noted,is selling at less than £60,000. Brownhill Extended was less than a month since S?iiing. at £25,000, whereas to-lay's market value i: over £400,000. The same good fortune may await Hannauss Be:le View shareholders.—" White- hall Review."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <p>ANCIENT ORDER OF FORESTERS.</p>
        <p>THE PAST HIGH CHIEF RANGER AT WARRINGTON. CHURCH PARADE.</p>
        <p>Bro. Hulse, of Chester, Past High Chief Ranger of the Order of Foresters, visited Warrington on Saturday, and in the evening delivered a couple of addresses to the members of Courts 431 and 240. In each instance there was a large attendance of members. At Court 240, the half yearly meeting was held. Brother G. G. Griffiths was elected chief ranger, Bro G Allen sub-cruet ranger, Bro Catterall beadle' and Bros Amons and Reeves woodwards, Bro T 14 Smith sub-treasurer, Bro L Davies seers tary, Bro George Smith hon treasurer. Bro Hulse attended the meeting at Court 240 at nine o'clock, and was accompanied by several officers of the dis- trict, including Bro J. E. Troman, D.C.R. Bro 0. Maybury, P.D.C.R.</p>
        <p>_ Bro. HULSE in the course of an interesting address, spoke of the advantages to be derived from membership with the Ancient Order of Foresters. Its success to-Jay was evidence that the co-operation among ruen in the formation of friendly societies proved their worth to the nation. He exhorted every young member—and them were a good number present—':o take a deeper intern st in their court and not stop on the last rung of the ladder. Every member should take to himself the re- sponsibility for the SLIC3CSS of his court, and much de- pended upon self-denial and a strict sense of duty. 'raking himself for instance, during the whole 30 years he had been associated with the Order, he had never missed a single court meeting, ex:tept whan business re- quired his presence at the annual High Court meeting. If all could bring themselves to this real sense of duty, how much better it would be for the Order? These were the days of the survival of the fittest. Competition was so keen that only those who exercised great energy and threw themselves heart and soul into their work could expect to make headway. So it was with regard to friendly societies. Those who were backward and hesi- tated to do honest work round themselves practically left at the finish. Their aim should be to foster friend- ship and do all they could to assist these who were in distress. Referring to'ithe financial and numerical strength of the order, Bro. Hulse stated that there had been 50,000 secessions, a fact which he sincerely deplored. It arose through members not having been able to pay their subseriplons and to bereavements. He would advise that all courts should set aside a contribution so as to be able to assist such members over the tide of misfortune. When it was realised that the members comprised nearly a million, it would be seen what a great responsibility those in charge had to maintain the Order's present pß3ition in the face of competition. Reference was made to the financial con- dition of the Order, and the millions sterling it was worth. In 1597, it was state], over £BOO,OOO was paid for sick relief, bereavements andidistress, averaging nearly thirty-two shillings a minute for the year. He advised all to show a spirit of kindness, to help one another, to adhere to principles which were honest and true, and then success would be assured. Daring the reign of Her Majesty the Queen, twenty millions had been paid by their Order. In sickness, bereavement and distress the homes that had thus Leers made happy tes•i- , fied to the good work that was being done. Their nob'e Order must rank, if not first, second to none, and it was certain that their great nation, of whith they were , all proud, had reason to be thankful for the formation of such organizations as the friendly societies. He had de, ! rived very much pleasure from his visit to Warrington, and the young members whom he had met that night showed an interest in the work which deserved that success that was bound to cemo with energy and determination.</p>
        <p>A vote of thanks was passed to Bro. Hulse, on the motion of Bro. H. Maybury and seconded by Bro. Bul- lock, and supported by Bro. George Maybury and Thomas Smith, and a highly successful meeting was brought to a close. A church parade was held on Sunday. The members of the various Orders assembled in the Market Place, prior to taking part in the open meeting at the Wycliffe Church. There was a capital attendance of members, who wore their regalia, and headed by the Boroueh band walked by way of Sankey-street, Horse- market-street, and Bewsey-street to the church. All the officials of the district were in attendance,and the s procession was witnessed by a large number of people. The meeting at the church was presided over by the Rev. John 'Vane (pastor) who exteeded a hearty greeting to the members of the society. He stated that the visit was somewhat an accidental one. He had invited Mr. Hulse to deliver an address to the members of the P.S.A Society, and on it becoming known, the members of the local courts of the Ancient Order ef Forseters,determined to mark the visit of their past High Chief Ranger by a church parade. It was an unprecedented event in con- nection with that church, but he believed it was customary for some friendly secieties to go to church once a year. He did not think they were in the habit of going to the humble dissenting chapel. He did not know why, because Nonconformist ministers were always interested in working men and their institutions. He understood that the Order of Foresters had about 900,000 members, and the money invested in their names amounted to the splendid sum of £6,339,000. Front the report of 1897 he noticed that there were 26,525 on sick pay, and that during the year £BOO,OOO were paid for sickness, funerals, and benevo- lent purposes. These facts were very soon told, but how much they meant? How much they spoke of the valuable help and assistance rendered to men in times of necessity and trouble ? He believed all interested in the welfare of the working classes and in this nation ought to take a great interest and have a very great sympathy with the friendly s3cieties of this country, among which the Ancient Order of Foresters,if not the first, stood in the first rank. He should always be willing to help them in any possible way. Bro. HULSE then delivered a thoughtful address on " Life." All life he pointed out was the gift of God,and human life was the highest form of life. They were called upon to work out their own salvation, and he de- sired to ask them how they were spewing their lives ? If they were merely seeking wealth, position or fame then they had missed the true aim of life. The speaker proceeded to hold up Jesus Christ as their example. At the conclusion of his address. Bro. Hulse spoke a few words to the members of friendly so- cieties. He was proud to see so many present that afternoon. It was a beautiful thing to see members , of friendly societies gathered together not only in their National church but in a Nonconformist church. If they looked back they would see what a remarkable change had taken place in the position of friendly societies in ' this country. In the last century the Government looked upon them with distrust. Members of Friendly societies were shadowed and it would have been impossible for such a large gathering as that to have met together. But in spite of all opposition workingmen had banded themselves together to such an extent that their friendly societies were now one of the graedsst and most important features of the 'national life of!this ceuntry. They had taught the nation great lessons of self-help and thrift. No other nation in the world could boast of such voluntary institutions as were to be found in England. Friendly societies in England were mar- velled at by their brethren on the continent. They failed to see how it was poseible for so many of the working-classes of England to be banded together for mutual help in times of sickness and distress. The speaker proceeded to refer to the beneficial effects of friendly societies upon the members themselves in mak- ing them better citizens. He attributed much of the greatness of the British nation to its friendly societies. During the Queen's reign he believed no less a sum than £100,000,000 had teen paid by friendly I secieties in cases of sickness, distress and death. There was now being paid £lOO,OOO every week by the friendly societies of this country and he wondered where that large sum of money would COllls from if workingmen themselves had not provided for it. Hes believed the friendly societies were a great safety valve for the nation and were the means of keeping it in the first niece. He concluded by urging the members of friendly societiee to be staunch and true to them, for by doing their duty they would be helping to make the world a little brighter and better than when they entered it. Miss Lawless (contralto) and Mr. Ainscoueh, of Wigan (tenor), sang solos. Miss Lawless was heard to great advantage in the solo " Dream of Paradise," and Mr. kinscouch, in his pure toned and cultured tener voice Pang "In Native Worth "from "The Creation." Mr. W. H. Payton, Mus. Bach., presided at the organ. The . service was also taken part in by Mr. Lang and Mr. B. 1 Farrar. A collection was taken on behalf ot the P.S.A. expenses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BURTON.</head>
        <p>BURTON.</p>
        <p>THE SID DEA.TH OF MR. CHARLES</p>
        <p>THE INQUEST.</p>
        <p>- Mr. J. E. Worsley, on Friday afternoon, held an in. quest at the Loudon and North-Western Hotel, Lew lands-road, Runcorn, on the hoop of Charles Burton. Samuel Pearson, clerk at the Tide Office, Runcorn Decks.otated that,the deceased was his brother-in-law He was 23 years of age, and a law student serving articles with a Warrington solicitor. He had been staying with witness at Ir well-lane during the last three weeks. Shortly after ? o'clock on the evening of the 7111 inst. witness left him at home, and proceeded to the Pierhead to see about his dillies. At that time Burton was in his usual health and stated that he would come down to the office to see loin] later. Subsequently he heard that la man had fallen into the !oars!, and was very much shocked to learn that It was his brother-in-law. He (witness) was of opinion that inasmuch as there was only a footway across he lock gates on one side, deceased being inexperienced in.such things had ab- tempted to cross on the wrong side and so fallen into the canal. The night of the accident was rather dark. It was not usual to have lights on the lock gates during June or July; but had the lights been burning he (wit' nese) was of opinion that deceased would have been able to see his way and the accident would not have happened. No one had a right of way over the gates and the footways were simply for the convenience of the boat-people. The general public were, how- ever, in the habit of using them. If lamps were kept burning all the year round, but especially on dark nights, it would not only be better for the general public but also for the people employed plying boats.—T.he Foreman (Mr. Robert Wright) ; Are not the lamps Mat tide time P—Witness : Not that I am aware of. It the other side—the south side of the canal—where the craft go, there are, of course, always lights.—A Juror : It seems to be the custom throughout the whole of Run- corn to do away with lights during June and July.— Further questioned witness said he did not think that it would cost the company much more, to have the light burning on dark nights in June and July. Captain William Johnson, pier-master at the Runcorn Docks, said shortly after 11 o'clock on the 7th inst he heard that a man had fallen into one of the locks on the old line of the Bridgewater Canal. He gave orders to stop the traffic and went and assisted in the recovery of the body, which :was successfully accomplished about half an hour afterwards. Every effort was made to re- store life. Witness with assistance undid deceased's clothing and he was turned on his side but there did not appear to be a vestige of life in him. He thought that the young man would have been in the water about half an hour, and tiat there was no chance of using the restorative means of the ambulance society. With regard to the custom of the lighting of the lamps he corrobor- ated the first witness, and also agreed with him in the theory that the deceased must have fallen into the cella in trying to cross the lock gate on the wrong side. What confirmed this opinion was the fact that deceased's body was found on the wrong side of the lock. The Coroner, in summing up, said it would appear that the theory seemed to be:: On one side of the lock gates there was a wooden footway and on the other none, and the deceased in eneeavouring to cross on the wrong side fell into the water and was drowned. That was the theory, only. It might be the fact; and he (the Coroner) was inclined to believe it was. It seemed that the place was lit, with the exception of the month of , June and July, all the year round. But in those months it was not conddered necessary to light the place. How- ever, according to the evidence of the brother-in-law and the last witness, had the lamps been lit in all probabiltiy the accident would never have occurred. There was no liability on the part of the Canal Com- pany, either to light the bridges or leave thema unlit, and if the public liked to go on the bridges over the lock gates they did so at their own risk and responsibility. At the same time, if they (the jury) thought that valuable lives— either of the public trespassing there or of the employe; of the Canal Company using the bridges—would be saved by the lighting of the lamps, from his exiarience of the Skip Canal Campany, he would say that any suggestion they made as to the lighting of the places would be duly considered by the company. Indeed, it seemed that it could be done at a little expence, as :a watchman was always there, and the gas could be turned off when day- light appeared, very easily. As far as that particular enquiry was concerned he cmsidere I that the only verdict they could return was" found drowned." The jury returned a verdict of "Found drowned," and recommended that the Coroner should write to the Ship Canal Company, suggesting that the place be lighted, if not during the whole 12 months, at any rate, on those nights when the darkness necessitated it. Much sympathy was expressed with the father and relatives, by both Coroner and jury.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>THE FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>The remains of the late Mr. Charles Burton, were in- tered in the Warrington Cemetery on Saturday after- noon. There was an extremely large attendance of the general public,and on every hand the sincerest sympathy was expressed towards the family and relatives in their painful bereavement. The chief mourners were Mr and Mrs Burton (father and mother), Messrs Thomas and William Burton (brothers), Mr W Burton (uncle), Mr W Burton (of Wigan), Mr Samuel Pearson (brother-in-law) and two younger brothers. Mr Forshaw (with whom deceased was articled), and Mrs Kashaw were also pre- sent. Mr H Ashton, secretary, represented the Warring- ton Football Club,and several members of the St. Mary's Club attended. The funeral service, both in the chapel and at the graveside, was conducted by Mr. William Hudson. The coffin was borne to tha grave by Messrs W Dilworth, H Houghton, C Burton (cousin), F Pearson Lawton, and Parkinson. Wreaths were sent by Mr and Mrs Forshaw, the Warrington Football Club, St. Mary's Club, and deceased's fellow clerks.</p>
      </div>
