      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>EXAMINEE,</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINEE,</p>
        <p>JUNE:iIBh 1899</p>
        <p>SIR ALFRED MILNER has A Policy of " Singular declared that our policy in Patience." South African affairs, as far 48 the Transvaal is concerned, has been one of "Singular patience" We are not concerned to question the remark, and probably it is as correct D. 5 any account of the situation can very well be. he attitude of the Boers has been one of obstinate self-defence. They imagine that their dominion ill in danger. The British subjects who have settled in the gold-bearing regions of the Trans- vaal are not contented with their position. They can trek, no doubt, but men cannot throw away ad the results of their mining labour. They can- not take the gold with them, if they could carry ItwaY the machinery. British capital is inves- ted in these mines, and British capital has made Johannesburg what it is. The taxation of British wealth and labour has kept the Transvaal solvent. All these facts we know. But now that the long-expected Blue-book has been issued the question of the moment is whether the Policy of patience is going to prevail and be success- ful. Mr. CHAMBERLAIN is said to be sanguine He has hoped for a long time that President *KRUGER would make concessions, and endeavour to treat the British in the Transvaal as if they Were local subjects and not a subject race. It oeeras to us that the aim of the BJers is to make all the British subjects in the Transvaal smart for the Raid and the doings of the Reform Committee. We have now been at work for two years endea- Vouring to obtain by pacific means some real amelioration of the political conditions under which the Outlanders are compelled to live Our restraint and pacific tendencies have been misun- derstood, and we fear there is little chance of any immediate change. President KRUGER'S friends in South Africa and in Europe are advising him to be generous, and to propose something that can be accepted by the British Government. If be Were so disposed the older Boers would not give way. They are insolent and obnoxious. They do not like the idea of relinquishing any of their Privileges, and their enjoyment of the position of donainance and comparative freedom from taxa- tion. We do not think they mean to give way if they can help it. They are not easily reached by arguments which would appeal to more enlight- ened people. They seldom read newspapers, they do not care for moral pressure, and they have acquired a contempt for the British people and the British soldier. Here is the danger of the situa- tion, and we fail to see ;'how it is to be mended by Patience. Hitherto they have taken our patienc for a sign of weakness, and it looks as if PRESI- ISENT KRUGER were much of the same opinion. Ile has had a chance of showing a wise and prudent sPirit, and he has not do so. The Bloemfontein conference was a great concession on the part of this country. It is impossible we can carry our Patience much further. The idea of waiting for a time is wise ; but we cannot: let the matter drop, ws cannot suffer the Boers to play with us, and "r position in South Africa will become unten- able if we allow year after year to pass away and °lle form of negotiation after another to fail. The Nlicy of patience is not yet exhausted, however, !ad we do right to try it a little longer, letting the Boers know that it is only to be for a little °nger.</p>
        <p>• THE formation of a new bond Au Ariz lc-Irish batween England and Ireland Tunnel:</p>
        <p>is always a matter of interest. The projected tunnel could hardly fail to be a 'huk of this kind, and probably for that reason,:it hie ilot yet received any warm support from the liationalists, though their hesitation must not be Ileeeesarily adjudged to be hostility. The argu- leaents in favour of the scheme are very strong. There is a point between the two countries only %bout twenty miles in length,and though it would Le Connected with the Scotch railways we may for °tile() regard Scotland as synonymous with Eng- L'aild• The tunnel, if made at all, would -;4111Pe, in the main, to be made with 'eglish or, at least, Imperial money. The reent idea is to obtain a guarantee reel the Treasury on the estimated outlay of ten billlions. The cost of the tunnel, however, is only °lle part, though it may be a necessary part, of the 4bneral scheme. The tunnel would require to be eehnected with a better railway service than at Present exists at either end, and if the Westernl 15t0°48 and harbours of Ireland are really to benefit the extent desired and necessary if the tunnel t L'es become a paying concern, there would have beta considerable amount of money laid out in 0 c'eks, piers, and quays. The whole scheme is thus tell° of great dimensions, and it is not to be en- upon in a reckless and daring spirit. There he no doubt that the tunnel could be made, .411: that it would, with the required accessories, c'st revolutionise the existing traffic between</p>
        <p>England and Scotland and America. The time and coal it would save to use a harbour on the West Coast of Ireland cannot be accurately estimated off-hand, but it would be considerable. Liverpool, Greenock, Bristol and Southampton would be affected, and possibly the journey to Canada and America might be eventually shortened by twenty-four hours. One of the diffi- culties of the enterprise would be that of coal,and Welsh coal would probably have to be stored in large quantities at the port selected on the Donegal coast. The direct benefit to Ireland would be considerable. As it is, some American bound vessels call at the entrance to Lough Foyle, and there is a good deal of emigration from Moville. American goods and cattle would come to Ireland in increasing quanti- ties. The mere handling of freight would mean the employment of a good deal of labour. Ireland's prosperity must be affected for the batter, and if no injury were done to Liverpool, probably E ug- ish and Scotch interests would not suffer, though it can hardly be expected that the shipping firms and houses in Liverpool will be inert the moment the making of a tunnel from Stranraer to Larne becomes a practical question. The subject deserves discussion, and it may be that certain aspects of the project may not seem so favourable after they have been exhaustively examined. But on the first blush of the matter we should say that the tunnel is most desirable, and is likely to be of im- mense benefit commercially and industrially. The military aspect had better be kept in the back- ground at present.</p>
        <p>There is a saying that the eye sees only what it brings with it. None but those who have a sense of humour can appreciate wit in the Law Courts. Lord Esher,Lord Brampton (Sir Henry Hawkins), Sir Frank Lockwood, Mr. Murphy, have all passed from the legal arena within the last few years, and Mr. Oswald, Q.C., on account of ill-health, has not been so frequent a pleader as he used to be. There are no new wits, if we except Mr. Ju.tice Darling, who makes an occasional observation, and hence it must be that many a scene, or a fragment of conversation which would lend itself to a bright turn, will pass unnoticed. Brilliant and courageous man that he is, Lord Russell of Killowen would hardly dare to assume the ignor- ance of the late Lord Chief Justice, and ask blandly when the name is mentioned in a case : Who is Connie Gilchrist ?" Mr. Justice Day, with all his gravity, too, could hardly ask with composure, as did Lord Brampton, when it was said that a music-hall singer was being driven to a performance at the Palace: "What Palace ?" As for Mr. Oswald, everyone knows how he retorted on a judge now deceased. " I can teach you law, sir," said the judge, "but I cannot teach you manners." "That is so, my lord," re- plied the barrister humbly. To this has been made a pendant, which has, of course, been attributed to the same counsel. A judge, whose views were somewhat vacillating, had intimated, so the tale runs, that he should reserve judgment, or, to use his own unlucky phrase, that "he should take time to make up his mind." "And when," inquired Mr. Oswald demurely," will your lordship have made up what your lordship pleases to call your mind Of course no lawyer would fly in the face of ermine in that way; but the legend is ben trovato.</p>
        <p>Not very long ago we had occasion to comment on the ineffectiveness of the Inebriates' Act, owing to the lack of homes or reformatories for the habitual drunkards. There are only three such places in the country, all of which are for women, and the majority of County authorities have,so far, shown little disposition to make the necessary provision for that unfortunate class of people—the habitual drunkards. Lancashire, we are very pleased to note, is awake to its responsibilities in this direction, and with praiseworthy entei prise leads the way in the construction of three homes, two for women inebriates, and one for men. The decision to take this step was come to at a confer- ence of the authorities on Monday, and the ex- pense will be borne ratably on the basis, of the Lunatic Asylums Act. A rough estimate gives the number of" habituals " for the county of Lancaster at 667, an estimate which may be somewhat excessive. Accommodation will be pro- vided for the number of cases expected during twelve months, and we may thus expect, presently, to have society purged to some extent of those dreadful people addicted to the pernicious habit of drunkenness. Not only will the public at large be benefited, but the inebriates themselves will be treated with such care and patience as is certain to result in the re-claiming of some few from the depths to which they have fallen. We cannot help but remarkon the melancholy necessity for such homes, and those otker institutions of a similar class, lunatic asylums. The advances of civiliza- tion only seem to be followed by a corresponding increase in the number of the outcast and insane. Social reformers have a huge task before them,but they should not be daunted by it. Their work is not without encouraging results, and the carrying into effect of the Inebriates' Act is one which they may congra.tulate themselves upon.</p>
        <p>The Lancashire County Council, we are pleased to notice, are insisting upon the Newton Urban District Council taking steps to efficiently trdat the effluent from their sewage works and thereby prevent the pollution of Sankey Brook. Pressure is also being exercised in the same direction by the Local (..4ov3rnment Board, who recently, through their inspector, roundly condemned the sanitary arrangemerts of the Newton Council. The course adopted by the Newton authority at their meet- ing last week cannot be said to show an earnest desire to comply with the wishes of either the Local Government Board or the Lancashire County Council. We should have thought that the members of the Council were by this time sufficiently well acquainted with the quality of the effluent they are turning into Sankey and Newton Brooks without being under the necessity, of calling upon their Surveyor to supply such in- formation. It is well known that there are other authorities higher up the stream who are doing exactly what Newton is daily perpetrating, for, as a matter of fact the stream is in a polluted con- dition before it reaches the Newton district. But this surely cannot absolve them from efficiently treating their own sewage, and the sooner the Newton authorities make up their minds to take up the matter in a business-like way the better it will be for the health of the district and they will set a good example to neighbouring authorities</p>
        <p>...-•,..•4101.-..... LArCRFORD GOOD TENIPLARS.</p>
        <p>The weekly meeting of the Latchford Good Templars was held in the above hall on Monday evening. The chair was occupied by Bro. J. Corbett chief templar. After the opening ceremonies had been gone through, ehort addressss were given by the members on various subjects One member was re-instated. Avery pleasantevening was spent, and the meeting was closed with singing and prayer. SUNDAY SCHOOL SHRMOgS AT PRNSTON Brtoox. The annual sermons for the support of the Pres- ton Brook Sunday school to* place at the Wes- leyan Chapel on Sunday, when two sermons were preached by the Rev. J. A. B. Malvern, of Runcorn. The services commenced at 2-20 and 6-30, and at the latter servics the chapel was filled to its utmost capacity. Special hymns and anthems were credit- ably rendered by the choir and friends, and at the conclusion of each servic3 a collection was made in aid ef the Sunday school funds, the whole amount- ing to £9 8s 2d. On Monday afternoon the scholars attending the Sunday school were given their annual summer tea in the chapel schoolroom. The following teachers were present, assisting in promo- ting the enjoyment of the youngsters :—Miss R. Webb, Miss E. Jones; Messrs. Johnson (superinten- dent), J. Jackson, and James Webb. After tea had been partaken of the scholars repaired to a field kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. B. Scragg, where games en 1 contests of every suitable kind were eagerly indulged in until dusk, when the pleasant evening was hr-Hight to a close. The Pres- ton Bro.* brass band was in attendance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY JUNE 16, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY JUNE 16, 1899.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>On Saturhay,the run of the above club was to Beeston Castle. by way of Acton Bridge, Weaverham, and Con- nineram. After an enjoyaMy spmt afternoon, the re- turn was made through. Whanington and Tarvin. A goxily namber turned out and the run was much en- joyed.</p>
        <p>MILTON P.S.A., WIDNES.</p>
        <p>At the ordinary P.S A. service on Sunday there was a good attendance. The chair was occupied by Mr. G. Lacey. who spoke upon the true manner of reading. The Rev. J. Pedlar followed with an address upon 'Books" and gay some capital advice upon the choice of books from the prize selec,dons. Two solos. "Angels ever bright and fair" and "Tired," were rendered in a pleas- ing manner by Miss Taylar of Runcorn.</p>
        <p>SUSPECTED RABIES AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>An interesting case to dog f traders has occurred at Runcorn. Samuel Johnson, of Speakman-street, had a terrier dog which strangely attacked him on Sunday afternoon. He brought in Constable Rilson, who was also attacked. The animal then jumped down a close, but returned to the atta-:k, and Johnsan killed the animal with a hatchet. Mr. Ferguson,veterinary surgeon, Warrinuton, made a post-mortem on IVl,ntlay, and certi- fied th.t the dog suffered not from rabies but from a fit consequent upon eating new potatoes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>WOMEN SENT TO GAOL AT RUNCORN. Before Mr. F. Wiswall on Tuesday merning, two women, Ann Radcliffe and Margaret Antrobus, were brought up in custody ; Radcliffe, for being drunk at 8.20 p.m., on blo-nday was fined ss: but Antrobus, whose offence was sleeping out at Dutton, on Tuesday. was sent to prison for 14 days.—The cases were proved by P.C.'s Smith and Ridgway. POLICE COURT MISSION. A street collection was made in Widnes on behalf of the above mission on Saturday. The arrangements were made by Messrs. Henry Taylor (Police Court Mis- sionary, Warrington), A. Kenyon, and R.C.L. Bingham, assisted by the Church Lads' Brigade and a number of ladies. The amount realised was £lO 3s 9J. WIDNES DAY SCHOOLS ANNIVERSARY. The anniversary services in connection with the Widnes Church thy schools were held on Sunday in St. Paul's Church. There were good congregations. The Vicar preached in the morning, and in the evening the Rev. Canon Dentin Thompson, 'M.A., Southport, occu- pied the pulpit, and gave an excellent discourse on the Seem .n on the Mount, in the course of wh:ce the rev. gentleman referred to the instruction given to the chil- dren, dwelling' specially upon the religious instruction which was imparted. Special anthems were rendered by the choir, the soloists being Masters H. Tebbutt and T. Hughes. In the afternoon the Rev. J. Adams, M.A., conducted a special scholars' service. Mr. Cooke, Run- corn, was the organist. " NORM'S " PHILOSOPHY. At the Runcorn Police Court, on Saturday, before Mr. F. J. Norman (chairman) and Mr. Walter Timmins, Henry Cooke, alias " Nobby "a well known local char- acter, was charged with disorderly behaviour in Poole. lane on the previous day.—P.C. Fry proved the ease.— Isaac Ellwood a boy, residing in Stanley-street, corrc- boreted.-4'he prisoner said with him it was a case of "give a dog a bad name and it would stick to him." He wiilved the Bench would send him away rather than he should be brought time time after time. The Chairman : Yen are a very disgraceful fellow.and have been charged with a similar offence before- You will be fined 403. and costs, 75., or one month's hard labour. " Nobby " decided to take his time. VOLUNTEER S.ND BOYS BRIGADE PARADE AT RUNCORN. On Friday evening the members of the Runcorn detachment of the 2nd V.B.C.R , accompanied by the Church Lads' Brigade and the several companies of the Boys' Brigade, marched to a field in the Holloway, where they were put through a number of battalion movements by Major HarrissoneThe boys drilled remark- ably well, and their performance excited the admiration of a large attendance of spectators. After the manceavres were completed they marched past in smart style. Volunteers and boys again formrd up in march- ing' order and returned to the Drill Hall, led by the buglers of the Church Lads Brigade. The performance of the bugle march, for a first attempt, was tayourebly cemmented upon by all present. The beys were trained by Private F. W. Walker, of the Volunteer Band. The Boys' Brigade was in charge of Captain Woodland and Lieutenant Leach, while the Church Lads' Brigade was under the command of Lieutenant Smith. ATTINIPTED SUICIDE AT WIDNES. On Friday, at the Widnes Police Come, before Alder- H. S. Timm's, Francis ' O'Neil, aged 20, 51 Pitt-street was charged with having attempted to commit suicide on Thursday, by hanging himself with a rope from the rafters of the closet.—Patrick O'Neil, uncle to the prisoner, said that about 11.30 p.m.. on Thursday he went unstairs. Prisoner then went downstairs, and wit- ness next saw him standing at the door of the closet. Witnesi's wife called out a few minutes after that she thought prisoner was catting his throat. Witness went out and saw prisoner standing in the doorway of the closet, with a rope round his neck, which was tied to the woodwork over head. He called out for help, and his nephew' Thomas came and cut Francis down, while witness i :held him up. Witness could not say f prisoner's feet were off the ground, but he was nearly conscious. Prisoner had a knife in his hand.—ln reply to Mr. Lewin (deputy magistrates' clerk), prisoner said that he knew nothing at all about the affair.—Patrick O'Neil, replying to the deputy magistrates' clerk, stated that prisoner had been drinking for some time. Witness had not seen any difference in him during the last two or three days. Prisoder had never threatened to do such a thing before. —The Bench remanded the prisoner to Walton gaol till Thursday. A PROFESSIONAL BEGGAR SENT TO PRISON. A's the Widnes Pollee Court on Monday before the Mayor (Alderman J. W. Wareing) and Alderman S. Sadler, Thomas Jordan was charged with begging on Saturday.—P.C. Fliperoft stated he saw the prisoner going from shop to shop in Victoria-read, begging. When asked what he was tieing, prisoner said that he vnust do something for a living.—Prisoner stated that only last Monday he came out of the hospital at Liver- pool, aid was making his way to Manchester. He had to rest a couple of days after he came out of the hos- pital.—lnspector Strickland said there was no doubt prisoner was a professional beggar. At one of the shops in Victoria-roed prisoner said that he was a shoemaker, and at another that he was a tailor. When he was brcueht to the station he was in drink, and he said that be must do something for a living. He also stated that he vesuld not work.—P.S. Carter corroborated.—The Chairman told the prisoner they had been inclined to deal with him leniently,until his tale had had another version given to it. He evidently did not know what the truth was. Ho would be sent te goal for 14 days' hard labour. DEFRAUDING THE LONDON AND NORTH-VVESTERN RAILWAY COMPANY. At the Rencorn Police Court, on Saturday, before Messrs. F. J. Norman and Walter Timmins, Albert Peck and Eloch Verne were charged on remand with travel- ling on the London and North-Western Railway with- out having previously paid their fare, and with intent to clefrand,on the Bth inst.—Detective Inspector Atkins, of Liverpool, stated that when the prisoners arrived at Ditton Mr. Bailey, the stationmaster, went to the cont. pa.rtment in which they were seated and asked for their tickete. They pro itaceel tickets which had been issued at 0xf0r.4.-road for the Cricket ground at Old Trafford, and which would cost above 3d. With these they had travelled to Ditton, and when aske 1 to pay their fare they said they had no money. Mr. Bailey took the tiskets from them. At Runcorn Station they were seen loitering and dodging about and a porter asked for their tickets. They said they had given them up at Ditton, Mr. Prescott, the stationmaster, asked for their names and addresses, and they gave several, all of which were , incorrect. He then gave them into custody. He (Mr. Atkins) did not wish to prefer against them the second and more serious charge, of giving wrong names and addresses.—The prisoners pleaded guilty.—Suoerintend ent Pearson said the prisoner Peck was a Runcorn man. He had been before the court previeusly, but he was ' then reprimanded and discharged, the reason being that had he been convicted it would have deprived him of a pension.—The prisoners were each fined 10s, and er. costs, or 14 days' hard labour. THE POLICE COURT AND PRISON GATE MISSION. A district collection on behalf of the Police Court. and Neon Gate MiS3lOO took p 1 OR Saturdayeat Widnes. Stands were conspicuously placed in the principal parts of the town, and were taken charge of by a number of local youne ladies.chieflv teachers from the Church day schools, who had kindly volunteered their services, aia the asopdi'sPetras bYeycl. Valuablecf'ng'derab alaesnsi7ancyie w n soliciting also aid frem ho- rendered by the Widnes Boys' Brigade, including No. 1 Company from St. Mary's and No. 2 Company from Si. inebrose. The boys were under the command of the Captain, Rev, R. E. Corle't. Between 30 and 4.0 boys of the Brigade were supplied with collecting boxes, and respectfully solieted contributiens from the public. The main body marched in procession through the thoroughfares. Every , assistance was given to the effort by ths police ; Super- intendent Kei,ehley kindly permitting the police station to be used for the distribution of the boxes and for the counting up of the proceeds. kdessrs. H. 'Taylor, A. Kenyon, and C. E. Bingham, Police Court missionaries, organised the arrangements. The Mayor (Alderman J. W. Wareing, generously regaled the boys of the two brigades with tea, • which was served at MTS. Braces, Victoria-road. The proceeds for. the day amounted to £lO 45.. nearly £8 of which vise in copper. WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.—CLOSING OF Two BURIAL GROUNDS. The monthly meetingmonthly of Widnes Town Council was held at Widees Town Hall on Tuesday afternoon, the Mayor (Alderman J. W. Wareing) presiding. The cemetery committee's proceedings stat ad a letter, dated 19th of May, had been received from the Home Office intimat- ing that it was the intention of the Home Secretary to represent to her Majesty in Council that for the protec- tion o! the public health no new burial ground should be opened in the parish of Widnes without the previous approval of one of the Secretaries of State, and that burials should be discontinued in St. Mary's Parish burial ground, forthwith and entirely, except under cer- tain conditions,one of which was that bueals be allowed in existing graves of the relations of those already is- terted therein, provided the bodies can be deposited at or below the dept of fiva feet without exposinr, coffins or disturbing human remains. Also that burials be die. continued in St. Berle's Boman Catholic graveyard, Ap- pleton, except that in the reserved space burial may be allowed of the Very Rev. Dean Finegan.at his disease in vault or grave not less than five feet (leap. The Com- mittee recommended that a representation be made to the Home Secretary asking him to reconsider his dee- i son, in so far as it requires an arbitrary depth of five, feet sbnve bodies interred in existing proprietary graves in St. Marv's Churchyard, the Committ• e being of opin- ion that a dept of three feet is sufficient for sanitary purposes, and else in respect to the closing of St. Berle's burial ground without allowing to owners of proprietary grevee there like privileges to those to be still At,l Iced by eraveewners in St. Mary's Chureloyard.--Aldsrman i Williamson n moving the adoption of the recommeele- tion, said that if their sugeeetinn were adopted 2,000 people could still be buried in Se I Mary's Churchyard, and in Se Bede's graveyard there were Over 100 unfilled proprietary graves. —Councillor Hemingway second el, and after a long discussion tee rcc Intrendati on was i adopted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>HANDSOME GIFT BY SIR JOHN BRUNNER.</head>
        <p>HANDSOME GIFT BY SIR JOHN BRUNNER.</p>
        <p>On Tuesda.y night the Moulton (Gle,hire) School Board discussed an extraordinary difficulty arising out of an order of the E lunation Dopartment that a deaf and dumb boy in the district shall attend a snecial school. As Moultms is a school board district expense would b en- tirely local, and wJuld represent a rate of in the pound. Otherwise the entire union would bear the burden: The clerk re.portod that Sir John Brunner bad coo ulted Sir George Kekewich with the object of intro- ducing a bill to remove the anomaly, and maantiml Sir John offered to boar the Coot. The offer was chankfay accepted.</p>
        <p>" SAVING A FLATMAN'S LIFE."</p>
        <p>At the Runcorn Policu Cou,t, on Wedn9sday,hefore Messrs. G. L. Davies, 'and S3muel Taylor, Willhnn Cheetnam, a flatman, of Liverpool, was brought up in custody charged with beiug drunk in Percival's-lane, Runcorn, on the previous day.—Prisoner pleaded guilty. Bratt said be observed the man going in the directicn of the docks the wor3n f.,r drink. Con e• siderin it was dangerous to allow the man to go down the dock's in that condition he locked him up.—lnspector Hicks stated A had becom3 n'cessary to keep a strict watch over people going in the direction of the docks owing to the number of deaths which took place there.—The Chairman observed that prisoner should be grateful to the officer ; as possibly he had been saved from a worse fate.—Fined 53 or 7 days in default. Sr. PAUL'S YOUNG MEN'S SOCIETY CLASS.—ANNUAL PIC-NIC.</p>
        <p>The members of the above class and their lady friends, numbering in all b'tween 70 and 80, had their annual pic-nic on Wednesday. The locale of the event was at Williams' Fcarm, Helsby,a,nd thither the company was conveyed from Runcorn by special conveyances. The pic-nic had in prsvious ymrs been held on the Whit Tu9siay,but the unfavour ible weather prevailing during the last two Whitsuntide holidays caused the member 3 of the class to await a more favourible opportunity. Favoured with a gloriously fine day:cite company thorough lv enjoyed themselves. The tei was provided by Mr. J. Nancollis, and the tea tables were presided over by Miss Uns worth and Mrs. Nancollis. Messrs. John JONOS and P. Unsworth also a,sisted the ladics, many of whom wore also indebted to Mr. E. F. Baxter for the hearty way they enjoyed themselves. The whole of the arrange- ments were in the hands of Messrs. Wesley, Coventry, and S. Dutton. and were well executed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES,</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES,</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT I What people are asking is :—Whatever has become of the rosy pictures that were held up to us, about 12 months ag), respecting the "transmog- rification "of the Runcorn Heath and Common ? When are the public baths going to be pushed for- ward ? Where and whatever has become of the Runcorn Cricket Club? And where restAh the spirit—that Burns so well expresses : " Breathes there a man with soul so dead; who never to him- self hath said : this is my own my native land,"— the spirit I say that should make Runcorn Runcorn and a home for the "rd-Ails," ?</p>
        <p>And what people ai'6 rtaying is :---The pictures have faded before the ominous X. s. d.--the fatal solver of so many problems ! The advent of the baths is lost in the dim vistas of the unexplored future ! The Cricket Club too has sunk into utter oblivion! There's "no nothing "and no spirit to teach us anything ! Like the fagot, for want of a circling brand; so the community of Runcorn,for the want of those rallying points which affard in- tercourse for man with man, lie scattered, to the detriment of town and trade.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn Anglers Asociation commences its slagon to-day (Friday). They have acquired the tights of some good waters ; and during the close season have had them well stocked with trout, breme, tench, carp &amp;e., Sze., which should result in good sport during the season. The club have also secured sp3cial concessions for travelling on the London and North Western Railway to and from the waters of the Anglers' Asiociation. From the evidencesfdiscern:ble there should be some good fish- ing, but on one point we may be assured ; there will certainly be some " capital yarns."</p>
        <p>The Runcorn School Board have awakened cen- iderable intereq In the town respecting the Granville-street Schools, by their recent discussion; and their deliberations-will for the future be closely watched. The denominationedist section (who are the ruling! majority) will be kept up to scratch, and notwithstanding my well-meant suggestions of settling the differences—a la box —further develop- ments are pending.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>SPIRITUALISM</p>
        <p>The speaker at the moetings of the Spiritualist Smiety on Sunday was Miss Cotterill and the even- ing address,on" Spiritualism—what is it ?" was in- teresting, Mr. J. T. Ward, of Blackburn, an old worker in the movemcmt, presided at the evening meeting, and in his opening remarks said he be- lieved that a knowleige of the facts and philosophy of Spiritualism was calculated to give us a fuller knowlsdge of ourselves, of our duties and responsi- bilities, wad of our relation to our Maker than any other religion. On Monday evening Miss Cotterill gave a number of clairvoyant psychometric delineations.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>VOLUNTEER FUNERAL.</head>
        <p>VOLUNTEER FUNERAL.</p>
        <p>On Saturday, at. the Warrington Cemetery, the remains «if Private M. Kolly, of C Company of the Warrington Volunteer Corps (who died the pre- vious Wednesday, of an internal complaint, after a short illness), were interred with full military honcurs. A number of volunteers under the direc- tion ot Captain Appleton, commanding the com- pany, met at the Drill Hall at two o'clock, and healed by the Volunteer Band, under the direction of Bandmaster Armstrong, marched to the late resi- dence of the deceased in ICirtz-street. Here they were joined by the mourners, and the procession having been reformed, a move was made for the Cemetery, where the service, always an impressive one, was gone through. Two buglers blew'th a long reveille, the firing party, under the direction of Colour-Sergeant Moore, concisting of 13 rank and file. The deceased was held in much respect by his volunteer comrades. MASONIC INSTALLATION.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the " Gilbert Greenall " lodge of the Ancient Free and Accepted Masons, No. 1,250, held at the Assembly Roonas,Lion Hotel,on Tuesday afternoon, the occasion being the 30th annual festival of St. John Baptist, Bro. John Robert Griffiths, S.W.. W.M-olect,was installed as Worship- ful Master. The ceremony of installation was per- formed by Bro. E Rose, P.Pr.D.G.C. The following officers were invested :—Bros. John Robert Griffiths ; William Chantry, I.P.M. ; Thomas William ; W hittaker, ',T.W. ; the Rev B Percival, chaplain; William Taylor. P.M., P.Pr.G,P. treasurer; Edward Rose, PM, P.Pr. D.G.D.C., secretary; James Parkinson, S.D ; John W G Craddock, 3.0; Peter Edgar, Organist ; John Emson, I.G ; William Cap- pock, senior steward; W Hodgkinson, junior stewarl ; J J Atherton, first assistant, steward ; C G NleDowell, second assistant steward ; William Richardson, tyler ; Bro. William Taylor, D.C. A banquet was served in the evening.and subsequently the usual loyal and masonic toasts were honoured. An interesting feature- of the proceedings was the singing of a masonic hymn, entitled " Hail ! 0 Master l" the words of which were by Bro. W H Jenkins, and the music by Bro. 3 Batchelder, and which has been dedicated by Bro Jenkins to the Worshipful Master, Wardens, and brethren of the lodge.</p>
        <p>YOU FIND IT EVERYWHERE. CUTICIIRA now meets the traveller at every point in tha civilized world. It seems like running across an friend in a strange land to find displays of CIITICURA and CUTICURA SOAP in the flvm windows of Melbourne and Sydney, Yokohama end Tokio, Hong Kong and Snanvh ti, Calcutta and Bom- bay. Constantinople and Cairl,Kimberlor and J,hannef- burg, Berlin and Vienna, Paris and London. Civiliza- tion seems to march with CUTICURA, in the front rank,.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>TRIBUTES TO THE LATE MR. PHILIP</head>
        <p>TRIBUTES TO THE LATE MR. PHILIP</p>
        <p>SPEAKMAN, J. P., C. A.</p>
        <p>At St. Michael's Church, Runcorn, on Sunday morn- ing, the widow and tatni.y of the let! Mr. Philip Speak- man, Fox Hill, Frodsham, attended divine service. Toe Rev. Canon Maitland Wood (vicar), who conducted the service, also preached the sermon. In the course of his remarks the rev. gentleman said he prayed to have something of the spirit of St. Barnaloas given to him that morning, in order that he might say something by way of consolation to the group of mourners who were among,t them, and renewing on a very sad occasion their connection with that church. It would almost be impertinence in him to attempt in any way to speak of the character of him for whom they mourned. Ile was much better known to the family and many of those present than he possibly could be to him. From what he had seen of him he tralieved he was a gentleman ot kindly heart and genial cheerfulness which could hardly have tailed to make him both amongst his own family and amongst others somewhat of a son of consolation. He could not help thinking that he must have been in his time a comfort to many. The preacher then offered a few words of consolation to the bereaved family. At the cicse of the service Mr. W. Ground a (the organist), played the Dead March in " Saul."</p>
        <p>THE RUNCORN URBIN COUNCIL'S TEiTINIONY.</p>
        <p>There was a melancholy group of Councillors gathered in the Town Hall, Runcorn, on Monday evening, collec- ted for the purpose of passing a vote of sympathy with tie widow and family of the late Mr. Philip Speakman. The profoundness of the occasion was apparent to all. Mr F Wiswall (chairman) presided and arra we:e also present: Mrssrs E Imison, C.C., William Handlev,Jam es Pritchard, J W Lightburn, G Hewitt, T Dean, T Jones, D Bisbrown, S Walton, with Mr F A Lake (clerk) and Mr E Marshall (secretary). The Chairman apologised for the absence of thrir re- spected Vice-Chairman, Mr. S. Taylor, who was pre- vented from attendance by circumstances over which he had no control. It was also intimated that owing to illness Mr. Vialter Collier could not attend.</p>
        <p>Upon commencing the proceedings the whole of the membe's r)se, and remained tandinj ufltil the close.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. Wiswall (chairman), said it was hi. duty—his mournful duty—that night to propose the following re- solution :—" That this Council do tender a vote of cen- dolence to the wife and family of the late Mr. Phi!ip Speskman, J.P., C.A.., on the great loss they have sus- tained by his death ; and begs to express its deep re- gret at the loss of a valued colleague, who for 25 years has sat on the governing body ot this town, and by his strenuous and enlightened action has done so much for the welt-being of his fellow-townsmen, and whose ripe judgment and experience will be sorely missed in the dehberations of this Council." In doing so he felt sure the r hears went out to the widow and family of their late lamented colleague, Mr. Speakman. The widow had not only lost a dear husband. INT, t long ago her eldest son, a young man full of the promise of a distinguished future, was taken away almost as suddenly as his father. The affliction must be a very severe one, and h. hoped that the widow and family would be sustained with fortitude. He knew the late Mr. Speakman for over 40 years; in fact they were boys together. They worked together and for the last ten years they had been closely connected with the govern- ing body of the town. They felt the loss very deeply, Mr. Speakman was full of energy and activity; he was always a busy man ; always at the post of duty, and he must have been greatly supported and sustained in his lifework by his wife and family, with whom they now desired to express their deep sympathy in the sad afilic- .tion which had bnfalen them.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. Pritchard said it was his melancholy duty to second the vote of condolence with Mrs. Speakman and family. By the removal of Mr. Speakman they had lost a valuable and energetic colleague. He blievel he first entered public life as far back 5q 25 years ago. He first acted on the local authority as a commissioner, and hid since contiuued his unvarying interest in the proceed- ines of the Council to the day of his death. He was remarkable for his untiring energy, and some cf them could call to mind with what vigour he took up any question affecting the interests of the Ceuncil, and by his spirit and effergy overcame obstacles that at first seemed insurmountable. The town had lost in him a true friend. As a supporter of every good cause he was ever ready to help forward any movement for the we- fare of of the community. He was always willing to assist any individual applicant who appealed to him to use his influence on his or her behalf. He attained positions of honour and usefulness in the county, anti though change of residence cline about, and Liverpool intaiiie pri6t..119 his hutinM Wig WO:ins, of remark that Mr. Speakman's interest in Runcorn never abated. He was ever concerned with the welfare of the town, and took a lively interest in all that was going forward. They all knew the public positions he held, and the manifold duties these positions involved, and that such an active career should be so suddenly snatshecl away from the interests of life was sal to contemplate. Mr. Liehtburn endorsed all that had been said of the late Mr. Philip Speakman. He had known the late Mr. Speekmen many years, His sterling chaTzvter, and the purity of his life and conversation wera such as might he an example to most men. He never heard an expres- sion from Mr. Speakman that he would not like pro• claimed throughout the world. His energy, his indo- mitable courage, had been recognised by all of them,and the town had been benefited to a very great extent by characteristics. He was quite sure they all sym- pathired very much with his amiable wife. He ceuld not sufficiently express his sympathy with her and the family, but he hoped they would be sustained in their severe and great trial. Mr. Bisbrown supported. He had been associated with the late Mr. Speakman for nearly 28 years, and was very much moved when he heard of his death Perhaps geniality was one of Mr. Speakman's most pro minent characteristics, and he thought they would all- eeree that he took a deep interest in local matters, and was distinguished for his public spirit. He filled every office he held with marked ability. They all sympathised very doeply with the family. Mr. Speakman worked himself up from a very humble position to the most lhonorable offices in the county, and in that respect he thought he had set an example to their young men. Mr. Imison, in further supporting the resolution, thought the chairman had correctly interepreted the feelings of the Ceuncil, and of the townspeople gener- ally, when he gave them that early opportunity of ex- pressing the sense of th .3 has they had sustained through Mr. Speakman's removal. As the chairman had re. marked,he had been a member of the local authority far over 25 years, and although of late be had not been so regular in his attendance as they could wish, yet he thought they always resognised that he was there when he was wanted. He alerays thought that the late Mr. Speakman was head and shoulders above any one of them in the conduct of any negotiations. In these I matters he exhibited a shrewdness,and a tactfulness,and a way of disarming oppesition—(hear,hear)—that many a time had done gold service to the town and the people whom he served. Mr. Bisbrown had said—and he quite agreed with him—that his characteristic was geniality. He always felt that he was a man who was inclined to see the best of everything and everyone. Hi; optimistic spirit and sunny disposition was something almost in- spiring, and when he took A thing in hand one had a sort of feeling that, let the result be whatever it 'nicht, they were thoroughly well represented in any negotia- tions in which Mr. Speakman took part. His interest in Runcorn was unatatehle. It had been, unfnrtuis- ately. a regrettable feature of many of their Runcorn worthies that when their connection with the town had been severed, their interest had ceased. But Mr, Speak- man certainly had none of that. He never forgot the claims of the place in which he spent the greater portion of his life, and he (Mr. Imison) doubted whether any good cause suffered from his removal. The Technical Institute, the scholarship scheme, or any other echenee, were always near to his heart, and he did not think any institutioe. either public or 'private, suffered or felt any perceptible loss by his removal of residence. When he was elected an alderman of the County Council, and ceased te directly represent the town on that body, his interest in Runcorn never waned. He (Mr. Imisen) was alwaes struck with how he peemecl to take Runcorn under his wing. He never lost an opportunity of advancing its chime, or working both in public and private for its interests. The respect in which he was hell by the County Connell was evinced in two ways, first by his election as an alderman, and, in a very melancholy way, by the very marked attsnd- ance—which many of them felt they ought at a great inconvenience to make—on the day of his funeral. To himself Mr. Speakman was kindness itself, and he cer- tainly went out of his way to show that there was no s3ro left after any coeest in which they had been en- gaged. He was a man who could ill b. spared, and he felt that the county and the town had lout a man whose place it would be exceedingly difficult to fill. Mr. W. Handley said, perhaps he had known Mr. Speakman for A longer Vme than anyone present. His family and Mr. Spealrman's family were very well knerrn to each other, and that intimacy had remained until the very last. The circumstances serrouncling Mr. Speakman's death were to him painfully sad. Only the other week he left the town for a short holiday, and al- most the last person be spoke to before going away— except the members of his own family—was Mr. Speak- man. He was then full of hope, full of intense purpose, and full of thoughts for the good of the town, as ever he had l:ren. He thought the longer they lived the more they would revere his character and nameeis would also those who lived after them. His distinguishing charac- teristic was his intense loyalty to the town of his adop- tion. He could never sneak too highly of Runcorn, and and he was ready to fight for its welfare at any time, and upon any occasion. Nothing he could say was too ninch to advance its interests. It was a true saying that "The deeds of a true man lived after hirra"and that, was indeed true of the late Mr. Speakmae. He joined most sympathetically in the mdancholy proceeding of passing a vote of condolence with the widow and family. The vote was unanimously passed. In addition to the floral tributes mentioned in our re- pert of the funeral of the late Mr. P. Speakman, a beau- tiful wreath was also sent by "The Committee and members of the Runcera Liberal Club."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>BEncirNc.—On Mondar before Mr. J. Ockleston, Eli, Smith, a tramp, was charged with begging from door to door in Helsby on the previous Saturday.—P.C. Needham proved the cis, and smith was committed for 14 days with hard labour.</p>
        <p>DETERMINED SUICIDE IN "SYNAGOGUE WELL."</p>
        <p>HEREDITARY FELO DE SE.</p>
        <p>Another startling case of suicide to-ik place at Froe.- sham, about 10 o'clock on Saturiav night, when Joseph Garner, brick- setter, of Cnurch-str,,et, Frodsham, drowned himself in the now too well known "Synagogue Well," which is situatei on the Castle Park premis.s. Sad to relate this is the f•-iurth member of the same family who has met his deathlia a similar manner, the grandfather, father, and uncle of the last victim all having been" found drowne-2i." __</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, Mr. J. C. Bate, coroner, held an inquest at the Frod-tham Sessions douse, on the bpdy of Joseph Garner. Mr. George Maddock was foreman of the jury. Dizabeth Garner, Church-street, said deceased was her son, aged 48 years. He was a bricklayer. The last time she saw him alive was at about five minutes past ten on Saturday night. He came into the honse and threw his cost and cap on a chair, and went out muttering something.</p>
        <p>John Fletcher, grocer's assistant, Church-street, said a few minutes past ten he was coming across the Town fields in the direction of Frodshain. He met Garner corning alonz the footpath. Garner spoke to him and asked witness VI go with him and see him drown him- self so that he could tell his mother. Witness nen tried to persuade Garner to go home, and got hold of him. Garner, however, put up his fist and knocked witness over a low hedge. Witness then hurried t ) the village and told Constable Wam.Y„. Constable Wagg said he went to the Synagogue Well with the last witness. He found the deceased in the w€ll. His head was but two inches from the top of the water. Deceased was standing in an upright position. He took him out and found that his ankles were tied together with his 'kerchief. He tried artificial respira- tion for about three-cparters of an hour, a': the end of which time Dr. Selby appeared and pronounced life ex- tinct. He searched deceased's clothing bit found no- thing but an old pipe.</p>
        <p>After a few minutes deliberation the jury brought in a verdict of " Suicide whilst teniporarily insane." A portion of the jury wer, in f tvour of recommend- in,g that a requisition be addressed to the owner of the properitv suggesting that the well be fenced off. The Coroner, however, observed that if it had been the accidental drowning of a child or something of that nature such a sugge3tion would be useful; but inasmu as the erection of a fence woald not prevent a grown- man committing suicide he did not see the utilits of it. 'roe auggestion was n-,t pressed. METHODIST FREE CHURCII BAZAAR. _</p>
        <p>The Town Hall, Frocisham, was quit; en .fcte on Wednesday aftirnlon, when a highly success:el bizaar promoted by the friends of the Methodist Free Church was held in that place. Some time ago,the cause,which is under the superintendency of the Rev. James Percival, decided upon renovating their church at a cost of £l,OOO, and in furthering that good object already some £345 have been accumulated ; and £95 promised towards the carrying on of the work.—The Rev. J. Percival, in in- troducing Mrs.F. Boston,of Cadogau House,Frodsham,to the task of formally opening the bazaar, dwelt upon her kindness in always readily assisting any good work. Ile would say, in explanation to the public who had been so generous to them, that the reaFon the work had not yet teen commenced was owing to delay caused by the architect. However, the delay had had some benefits, as during that time they were able to form an entirely new trtht, and now had a trust formed of working members of the society. (Hear hear). Also they had during that time accumulated more money. A review of the financial position showed they had receive i in round numbPrs £345, out of which they had been obliged to spend £6O ; that was,however, balanced by substantial premises, to the amount of £95. Some one had said tbey would do well if they realised £6O by that day's effort. He hoped, however,they would realise £lOO. As they were aware he would be leaving the neighbourhood, and his one hope was to leave the church in a satisfactory position. (Hear, bear). Flav:aiN commendei the articles!on the stall to the kletriblage-, Mr. Percival called upon Messrs. Noltla, and Thomas Earlarn, who proposed and secon,iN 4 Tote of thanks to Mrs. Boston, in ,antielphtion of her ser- vices that afternoon, and thanking her kr all past favours. The vote was carrlel with acclamation,—MtS, Boston, who waa heartily received ,on tising said : They had listened to Mr. Percival's fiß- ished speech with great interest. It was certainly very praiseworthy the way they had worked for that bazar and she hoped they wcrild receive a good amount for that work. She had had personal experience of bazaars and knew they were labours of love, and in the work of making and begging there was plenty of zealous church- work dane.She hoped that the zeal shown in their church might be shown in all the churches of Frodsham and that they might try to d 3 more for the evangelisation of the people. They must never rest satisfied,tut be always looking forth for fresh fields of labour and work. she was obliged to them for the kind remarks about herself ; but it wa; always a pletsure for her to b 3 present there. She had great pleasure in declaring the bazaar open,and wished them success beyond their best hopes. (Ap- ' plause.)—The stallholders were :—Sewing meeting stall, Mesdames J Percival, Jackson. and Maddock ; dairy stall, Mesdames Percival and Webb ; fancy stall, Miss Grice, Mesdames England and Ashton ; Sunday school stall, Misses A Norcross, Maddock, and P Beard ; flower stall, Mrs Jones and Miss Alice Jones ; refreshments,Mre Eirlam, Miss Ictiton, and Mrs Barnes; bran-tubs, No. 1, Misses Annie Maddock and Lizzie Mooton ; No. 2,Misses Hilda Beard and Elith Ellams.—ln addition there were the usual side shows, short concerts, curiosity shop, and also Mr. Bray of Widnes (phrenologist). The attendance was good throughout.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>RECTOR. OF FLINT AND TIP: LATE INIII.. T. E. ELLIS.</head>
        <p>THE RECTOR. OF FLINT AND TIP: LATE INIII.. T. E. ELLIS.</p>
        <p>AN ANSWER TO SIR J. T. BRUNNER: The Rev. W, Ll. Nicholas, rector of Flint, writes to the "Times "—The friends of Sir John Brunner have forwarded copies of his letter, which appeared in the 1" Times "of Wednesday last, to every Radical print in Wales, with a request that,in addition to the letter, souse 'alverso comments on my remarks should appear in their columns. The command has been ob,yed. Your well-known sense of fairr.ess, and the knowledge that you sent a commissioner to Wales to report on the anti-tithe war, which for a time raged with great viru- lence in some of the country parishes of North Wales, fanned by the leaders of modern Calvinism, of whom I need only mention the name of the Rev. Themes Gee, of Denbigh, are my only reasons for asking you to place before the public in a very condensed form the remarks I made on the fourth Sunday after Easter from the Epistle for the day (St. James i, 17). Having spoken of the Bible as one of God's great gifts, I briefly noticed the awful consequences resulting from hostility to the teaching of the Bible, as exempli- fied in the lives of individuals who had been under the influence of modern Calvinism, which is so bitterly opposed to the Bible in the elementary echools of Wales. To this sect I attribute the present (a) increase of dishonesty in %%ales ; kb) the prevalence of deceit ; (c) a readiness to accept any statement, without verification, if the Church can be injured thereby. (a) Under the first head I referred to the granite monument erected in the mountain parish of Llanarmon by modern Calvinism in memory of John Parry, who is credited with having started the anti-tithe movement in Wales. At the unveiling of the monument Mr. , Herbert Robertr, M.P., (a deacon of ined(re Calvinism) made use of this expression : "Religion was the k eynote of the life of John Parry." I asked what did Mr Roberts M.P., mean by religion ? Surely it was not the religion of Christ which said " Render unto Costar," .te. What Mr. Roberts ought to have said was" Modern Calvinism was the keynote of his life, and this ~monument is erected by modern Calvinism to the glorifihtion of dis- honesty." In the first lesson of the morning service of that day Moses ordered that nothing was to be added to or taken from the 'fen Commandments. One of these commandments said "Thou shalt not steal," and modern Calvinism by its erection of this mouument says " You may steal, but only from a clergyman." (b) Under the second head I referred to Mr. Thomas Ellis, M.P. I first mentioned that he had caught his death cold on the night of March 20,h, on which a blizzsrd raged, by remaining very late to support his friend Mr. Herbert Lewis (as Mr. Lloyd-George has pub- licly stated), who made a personal attack upon me, and was made the lauebing-stock of the House of Fommens by Sir John Gorst, Vte-President of the Council, who exposed the falsity of the chargos made by Mr. Lewis. I then refer:ed to the large amount of money left by Mr. Ellie!, a man who had always palmed himcelf off as a poor man before the farmers of Merioneth. I have told Sir John Brunner that I had never seen Mr. Ellie, and that my aft tek was directed against a system and not the individual—a system which had stunted the growth of true life in Mr. Ellis, who might have teen held up as an example to the you,h of Wales to all ages; but at preient Wales will not accept a deacon of modern Calvinism as a rationed hero. I may add that mode n Calvinism was worldly wise in startine the memorial ti Mr. Ellis on the day of the funeral, for the report of his wealth has practically dried up every fount of sup- port. This is the best answer to Sir J. Brunner. (e) Under the third heal I referred to Mr. Herbert Lewis, M.P. (a deacon of modern Calvinism), who brought forward in the House of Commons unfounded charges, and backed those eherees up by false dates, against the Flint National Schools. These chergee were supplied by Mr. Lewis's paid political agents in Flint.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>" INDECENT_PULPIT ATTACKS."</head>
        <p>" INDECENT_PULPIT ATTACKS."</p>
        <p>The" Daily Chronicie " allnhng to the—ln.cident says " We should not refer to the indecent pukit attacks upon the late Mr. Thomas Ellie but for lila light they throw upon the ferocious tone adopted by the Welsh Church towards Nomonformists. Mr. Nicholae, the Reotor of Flint, who writes to defend hie conduct in the " Times," charged Mr. Ellis from the pulpit with haying vlways palmed himself off as a poor man before the</p>
        <p>'farmers of Merioneth, and he alluded, in terms which we will not characterise, to Mr. Ellis having" caught his death-cold on the night of March 20, on whicn a blizzird raged" at a meeting at which Mr. Herbert Lewis attacked the Rector lf Flint. Mr. Ellis' as we learnt from Sir John Brunner's letter to the " Times" was independent of the financial support of the farmers, amongst whom he lived as one of themselves. But if he died possessed of a hundred thousand instead of eleven thousand pounds, what business, we should like to know, has a clergyman of the Church of England to drag a man's private affairs into the pulpit? To Englishmen the Incident is simply incredible."</p>
