      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES.</p>
        <p>THE LABELLING OF MARGAHINIC At the Warrington Borough Police Court on Monday morning before Mr. Birchall and other magistrate; James Taylor, 31, Moorley-street, was summoned for exposing, margarine for sale without having a label attached on May 26th.—Inspector Norman said defendant kept a provision shop, and on the day he visited the place the margarine was not labelled. The label was afterwards found in a drawer. The wife said the label had dropped off.—Defendant said the girl was cleaning the shop. She took the label off the margarine but forgot to put a clean one on. A fine of 10.1 and costs amounting to 184 was imposed.—William Peers, 68, Cloorley-street, was summoned for a similar offence on May I6th, and also for selling margarine not wrapped in the necessary printed paper —lnspector Norman said defendant also kept a provision shop,and he visited it on the same d ty. Defendant's wife told him that the margarine was a mixture. He told her there was no label on it. She pointed to a broken label, the only letters visible being " rine." It was necessary that the whole should be visible. He asked to be supplied with a pound of the margarine, and she gave it to him in a plain paper.— Defendant said the margarine was labelled. He pleaded guilty to the second offence. The Chairman said there seemed to be little doubt about the first offence. De- f flilant would be fined 101 and costs to cover both cases 2s 63 is all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>PROPERTY OWNER FINED.</head>
        <p>PROPERTY OWNER FINED.</p>
        <p>On Monday at the Warrington Borough Police Court before Mr. Thomas Birchall and other magistrates, Alfred T. Moult, 39, Princess-street, Manchester, was summoned for allowing a nuisancl to exist at a house belonging to him in Challinor-street.—Dafendant was represented by a clerk.—Walter Flood proved serving the necessary notice.—lnspector N-irman said defendant was the owner of a number of c )tt sges in Warrington. The cottage in question was 12, Challinor-street. On the 24th of last month there was a case of infectious disease at the house. An inspection was male of the place. The floor was broken up, and the house was in a most filthy condition. He should think it was barely fit for cattle to be housed in. Defendant knew of the condi- tion of the premises, but he had made no effort to do anything. He asked for an order and costs. They had a good deal of trouble with these people.—The Chair- man said there were many of these planes in Warringtoo and asked why the owncrs were not proceeded against ? In Dial Court there was some very bad property. It had been condemned, and yet it was occupiel.—An order was made for the nuisance to be abated in 14 days, de. fandant having also to pay the costs of the case (14s).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>MINOR CASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR CASES.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOROUGFI POLICE COURT. FRlDAY.—Before Mr:_ Birchall (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Councillor Smethurst. SUMMONS FOR MIINTENANCR.—Thomas M Williams,l9 Elm stElleemere Port was summoned to show cause why an order should not be made upon him for the maintenance of his wife. —Mr. Shearer said the wife had been In the Workhouse for some considerable time. He had received a letter from the defendant dated 24th January saying he was willing to pay, but defendant had only paid for a few weeks since that time. —Defendant said he had been in bad health.—By the Chair- man He bad no family and was willing to pay,—The Bench made an order for is per week and costs. PROPERTY OWNER SUMMONED.—George Henry Bottoms, 52, Manchester-road, was summoned for defective pail closet, etc.. at Ellesmere-street.—Mr. Norman said defendant was the owner of five houses in Ellesmere-street. At one house (N 0.5 there had been a case of infeotious disease (scarlet fever). The closet was inn very defecave state, The yard was also broken up and unpaved, and the house was in a most filthy condition. The defendant had been served with a notice on the 24th May, but he had made no effort to abate the nuisance. There WAS no cover for the ashpit. Defendant had been told about the nuisance many times before he got the notice but he made no effart until begot the summons.— Defendant said the work was finished the previous night.— Mr. Norman said it W 55 not, and he asked the Bench to itn• pose a penalty upon defendant as it was a very flagrant case. —Defendant said he gave the or ier for the work ten days ago but the builder gave up work without letting him know, —Mr. Norman said the work could be 'lone in seven days. The Bench made an order for the work to be executed in ten days and a penalty was imposed of 2s 6d per day after at time DESTROYING CLOYER.—Walter Siddall, 23. Sharp-street,was summoned for oommitting wilful damage to clover the pro- perty of Mr. James Newton, Orford-lane, to the amoant of 2s 6d. Isaac Whittaker was also summoned for the same offence the damage amounting to is--Police evidence was given in support of the charges.—Mr Newton said he had sufered "something scandalous" throagh lads going amongst the clover.—Defendants were fined in the costs of the summons in each case. " FOOLISH INTERFERENCE."—John Patifer Buck, milk dealer, 30. Allen-street, was charged with assaulting Rachel Buck, 1, Jolley-street, on the 3rd June.—r. A. Browne represented the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—Mrs. Buek said the defendant (her father-in-law) came in the house on the Saturday in question with her husband, and in consequence of a difference that had previously occurred after some bother, struck her while she was on the floor.— Mr. Browne said defendant had been brought by his son to the house, and he had no intention et committing an assault and he did not. They were obliged to restrain complainant, bility.—The defendant whow as excited. Mr. Buck was a man of the highestrespecta- (Mr. J. P. Buck)and Mr. Buick (corn- plainant's husband) gave evidence in support of Mr. Browne's statement.—The Chairman, after an adjourned consideration of the case by the Bench, said they had corns to the conclusion an assault had been committed. Mr. Bock should not have interfered hetweon husband and wife; it was a foolish thing to do.—A fine of 5s and costs was imposed, SATURDIY.—Before Messrs T Birchall (in the chair), R T Faireasueh, and J W Hayn-ard. ASSAULT.—John Whittaker and Walter Humphries were committed to gaol, the former for three months and the latter for a month, for assaulting a young woman named Annie Mahar in the early hours of Saturday morning at Arpley.—The Chairman severely commented upon Hum- phries conduct in not protecting Mahar from a brute like Whittaker. ALLEGED WIPP". ASSAULT AT WARRINGTON.—Thomas Rutter 28, Powys-street, was charged on a warrant with assaulting his wife on the 6th inst.—Complainant stated that on Tuesday evening her husband came home drunk and com- menced tot brow things about. He lifted her up by the neck, and it was not the first time he had done such a thing. The children were always frightened when he came in, and ran out to the neighbours. He was not safe in the house. She asked for a separation order.—Prisoner said that when he went home his wife hit him on the head with the poker, and made a mark which had not yet disappeared. He denied having assaulted her.—Mr. Taylor (Police Court Missionary) said that he knew the prisoner very well. His chief fault was that he drank. The complainant had a bad temper.— Superintendent Monaghan stated that prisoner had ben there four times previously-- The Chairman said they had decides' to give prisoner a chance if he would sign the pledge He advised Mrs. Butter to control her temper better.— Prisoner promised to sign the pledge and the case was ad- journed for a month. Morsbar.—Before Mr. Thomas Birchen (chairman), Alderman Blackhurst. Councillor Smethurst. and M . Wright. LICANSING.—W illiam Browne was granted temporary authority to sell at the Lower Seven Stars in place of Thomas Hatton, DRUNK AND DISORDERLY. —The following were fined for these offences :—lhomas Slater, 23, Kerfoot-street, general labourer, 5s ; Mary Broderick, 14th appearance, 14, Hay- dock-street. be and c, eta ; Patrick Purcell, farm labourer, Bank-street, 5s ; George Hopkins, 44th appearance. and James Hopkins, 22nd appearance, labourers, afelville-street, 5s and coats. lIRV.AKING A DOOR IN.—lsaac Smith, labourer, 23, Glass- house-row, was charged on a warrant with doing a ilful</p>
        <p>When asking for Cocoa, insist on hiving CinsußYS--sol nly i Packets and Tins as other COCOAS are often subati- uteJ ft r the sake of extra profit—At G)</p>
        <p>damage to a door, the property of Ely Jevons. on May2Oth. —Mrs Jervons stated that at half-past eleven on the night in question prisoner 0-itHe to her house and asked for his daugh- ter. Witness told him she was not in. He pushed his way into the house, and her son-in-law turned him out. He then began to use bad language, and kicked at the door breaking one of the panels. He said if he could get inside he would murder them ail. The damage to the door amounted to 12s Si—The Chairman (to prisoner) Why dida't you answer the summons? —Prisoner : It. wts during the holi- day week. I had no money and I didn't like.—Superinten- dent Monaghan said prisoner had'no money now. This was his 17th appearance.—Prisoner was fined 5s and costs and ordered to pay for the damage. THE 13 ENCII "THANKED."—John Gillies a stranger in the town, 'is sent to gaol for 14 days for begging in Wirtwick- street on Sunday night —P.C. 13 lw zds said he saw prisoner begging in Horsemarket-street, and cautioned him. The prisoner then went straight into Winwiek-street and stopped several people there.—Superintendent Monaghan said the prisoner showed no signs of having done any work recently, and was evidently a professional beggar.—Prisouer smiled on receiving his sentence. and said "thanks."</p>
        <p>OBSCENE INGUAGE.— Al b ert Stirrup,labourer,64.Acaderny- street ; Mary Quinn. 17. Philip-street ; Edward Mee,labourer,_ 3. Morris-street ; and Elizabeth Ellen Fleming. 2, Pillings court were fined for using obscene language.—Superintend eat Monaghan said the lagt-named defendant had been before the Court 51 times. She was the worst character in the town.—The Chairman said that young women like her were the despair of any court. He wished he could send her somewhere where she could be kept out of mischief. BREACH OF THE PrAcE.—Briclget Condliffe. Back Dial- street. and Bridget O'Brien, 11. Di a -street, were summone I for committing a breach of the peace by figting in Dial- street on June 3rd.—P.o Pennington proved the case. Con dliffe said they were only two mates having a fight.—The Chairman : Were you fighting for love ?—Detendant : No. —Defendants were bound over to keep the peace, and were Ordered to pay the costs. A CYCLIST FlNED.—Harry Clayton, 230, Manchester-road. was fined 2s 6d for riding a bicycle without a light at 11.15 on the aight of June 2nd.</p>
        <p>TuEsnsr-.—Befo-re Mr. I. J. Guest (pr esiaing), and Coun- cillor Smethurst.</p>
        <p>AN UNFJRTUNATS DEFENDANT.—JA mes Doyle, charged with being drunk at the Police Station at 12-15 the previous night. admitted having had some drink —P.C. Edwards said defendant came into the Po lice Station drunk, and annoyed Inspector Phillips who could not get on with his work.—Detendant said he called there to give information against a man for stealing from him. tie wanted a police officer to go, with him to get some evidence against the man. He mnearel the worse for driak through being upset. If he could have gone out of the station he might have found the mm with the money in his possessian.—Superinten lent Monoghan said they could make nothing of defendant's statement when at the Police Station.—The Beach dismissed the ease.</p>
        <p>" LIKE M.Ln DOGS."—Josepli Leonardy was charged on a warrant with not having appeared 0 his summons, for committing a breach of the peace. by fighting on the 29th April.—P.C. Edwards said defendant and his brother were fighting on the day in question in Pinners Brow.—They were more like mad dogs than anything else.—The magis- trates' clerk (Ar. White) said defendant should have appeared on the Btl4 May to his summons. Defendant's brother had appeared.—The magistrates said Leonardy would have to find a surety of .£5 to keep the peace, and was ordered to pay the costi, or in default would be committed for one month.</p>
        <p>WEDNESDLY—Before Alderman Sutton (presiding), and Mr Geddes.</p>
        <p>DRUNK AND FIGHTING,—Henry Poole. pleaded guilty to being drunk in Sankey-dtreet et ten minutes past six the previous night.—P. C. San tey said prisoner was fighting with another man. who ran away, and Poole refused his name and address.—Fined 5s and costs. _ _</p>
        <p>AVOIDING PAYMENT OF FARE.—Joseph Bernstein, a young man, was charged with attempting to travel on the London and North-Western Railway, from Warrington to Manchester, without paying his fare.—Detective Howells, in the employ of the company, said defendant was noticed on the station by a ticket collector, - He did not get a platform ticket, and after the collector had taken the tickets from the passengers, defenlant jumped on a step and got into a com- partment, bit the ticket collector and a porter got him out. Defendant told them he wanted to go to Birmingham, but he had no money, with the exception of a few coppers.— William Bennett, ticket collector, Bank Quay Statien, gave evidence bearing out this statement.—Superintendent Monaghan said defendant came from Manchester.—A tine of lOs and costs was i mposed, or 14 days in default.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN PETrY SESSIONS. MONDA.r.—Before Messrs. G. L. Davies (presiding), F. J. Norman, and W. Timmins. LlCZNSlNG.—Temporary authority to sell at the Sloop Inn, Lock-street, was granted to Thomas Harrison. Scaoor. CASES.—The following parents were proceeded against for neglecting to send their children to school, and on the evidence of School Attendance Offteer Bowyer, were flned 6d and 4S6A costs ; Alilltngton, Wellington-street. and Job Jones, senior. Boats, Runoorn.</p>
        <p>"NOT THE iI'IRST TIME." —Patrick Corcoran. of Penketh- lane, Runcorn. labourer, was a "little elevated" on the 3rd of June, when he was seen in Mersey-street by Constable Fry. Fry asked him to go home ; else he would be locked up. Corcoran replied ,"It would not be the first time." Subse- quently Police Se:•geant Gregory appeared on the scene, and took Corcoran home. The sequel was that Corcoran had to appear before the magistrates to give his account of the pro- ceedings, and admitting he was drunk was fined 53 including costs.</p>
        <p>REMAYDED WHILST A HOME IS PREPARED.—Elizabeth Barker, of Runcorn, was brought up in custody, charged with using obscene language in Poole-lane, on the Ist of June ; and with beinz drunk and disorderly in the same place, on the 10th June.—Prisoner pleaded guilty to both charges.—The first charge was proved by Constable Wilding, and the second by Constable Edwards.—Prisoner was re- manded until the 14th. so that enquiries could be made as to whether she could be accommodated in a home for inebriates.—Prisoner was brought up on remand on Wednes- day morning, and the magistrates (Messrs. G. L. Davies and Samuel Taylor) liberated her on her promising to go to a Woman's Home, at Chester.—Superintendent 1) Pearson Intimated that his enquiries about the home were satisfactory</p>
        <p>BUN AD WIFK.---Ileter Cloatsv orbit 15, liowArth-streei,• labourer, was summoned by his Wits, Margaret Jane Coats- worth, for using threats towards her on the 6th of June.— Defendant pleaded not guilty.—From the evidence of the wife,it appeared that defeudant.whi was her second husband, was in the habit of coming home drunk and had sworn to " cut her throat " Applicant, who was afraid of him, asked for a separation order.—The Bench suggested, the parties sheuld adjourn with a view to mutual arrangement. This was done, and shortly afterwar Is defendant and his wife intimated thAt an agreement had been come to. and beggei to withdraw the case.—The application was granted._</p>
        <p>CRUELTY To A. WIFE.—THE DIVORCE " dOUßT.—Fredrick Duckett, of Oobler's Lodging house, Fryer-street, was sum- moned by his wife, Emma Duckett, late of Cross-street. Runcorn, for using persistent cruelty towards her thereby causing her to live separately and apart from hino.—Defen- dent pleaded not guilty.—Applicant stated she would be satisfied if the children, of whom their were six under the age of sixteen, were provided for ; she was willing to work for herself. Her husband was in the habit of getting drunk every Saturday night, and treated her cruelly whilst in that state. On the Saturday before Whit-Sunday, he c true home with a " bottle of beer.'' After having a " few words " he struck her on the head with the bottle.—Cornelius Docket corroborated. Cross- examined the boy said "His mother did not strike his 'Pa,' but his 'Pa struck her with the bottle; aad he(the boy) when he saw the blood, ran out of the house calling ' mur- der l' There was no one else in the house at the time."— Defendant denied the allegation and said that he wanted to strike his wife, tut they both fell down. Whilst down, the eldest daughter took up the bottle and tried to strike him, but struok his wife in noistake.—Mr F J Norman said it was a very serious step the parties were about to take ; it was practically a divorce.—Applicant said she was determined not to live with her husband again.—The magistrates made an order on the defendant for 2s a week in respect of each child, and us 6d costs.—The defendant as he left the court said "YOU won't get it !" DESERTED THROUGH CRU ELTY. —Joseph Rovrston wa brought up, in custody, charged with being a deserter from the South Lancashire Regiment, Warrington, on the 22nd of May.—Police-Sergeant S Gregory stated he apprehended the prisoner, who resided with his mother at 2. North John-st., Runcorn, on the previous Sunday. He was in plain clothes at the time.—ln answer to the charge, prisoner said he had left his regiment about three weeks ago, on account of the cruel treatment he received from one of the Instructors.—The mother of the youth asked that the Magistrates do all they could to get the young lad out of the army ?—The Bench said they would remand him to await a military escort, when he would be handed over to the Military Authority. SLEEPING OUT.—A RUDE AWAKENING. —Mary Smith. who had walked over from Chester,was sleeping in an out-building in High-street, at about 12 30 a.m., on ths 12th lust, when she was abruptly awakened by Police Sergeant White. The officer however, provided her with a better lodging, viz., the police cells.—Later in the morning she appeared before the Bench and was eornmitted for 7 days at Kuutsford.</p>
        <p>Ladies' Walking Shoes,Great Variety, SCALIiS AD SONS, /3. Bridge-street, Warrington.—Advt. Police Uniform Boots. SCALES AND SONS, 83, Bridge-street. Warrington.—Advt- J. BAYLEY IgAsc offers SPECIAL, TERMS to SCEIOOLS for his splendid PORK PIES for Walking Day.—Advt.</p>
        <p>POTTED-- TONGUE, PRESSED BEEF, POTTED HEAD. BOILED HAAiS, ail kinds of cooked meats. J. BAYLEY ISAAC —Advt.</p>
        <p>LA.DIRS BST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER THE CLOCK, BRIDOR-STREET.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1899.</p>
        <p>ARPLEY 2ND V. PENFCETH FRIENDS' SCHOOL 2ND xt. —This match was played on the Penketh ground on Saturday, and resulted in an easy victory for Arpley by 57 runs. A. Dutton bowled well for the visitors, taking 6 wickets for 5 runs. Scores :—Penketh 12; Arpley 69.</p>
        <p>GRAPPENRALL V. FRODSRAM (SECOND ELEVENS).-Plaved at Grappenhall on Saturday. Score :-Grappenhall : Ford 3, Carter 6, Btnks 2s, Kirtland 0, Knight 1, Wi •ks 2. Wallace 0, Starkey 3, Fairhurst 14, Albinson 0. Hohln 5, extras 16. total 53. Frodsham : Abrams 0, Selq 3, Burrows 0, Wrigat 0, Ashworth 0. Gieave 6, Midge 0. Green 3, Forrester 1, Forster 0, Linaker 0, extras 2, total 15, PARR'S PINK V. HIGH LEGS -At High Legh. High Legh L H Gorton 1. 11 Elulme 28. 11 Clegg 4. G F 13-azert- dale 23, W E Farrer 13, Rev NV H F Wayne 4, C Hamden 1, Hon. J E Cross 26, T Renshaw 1. 0 Dwis 0, W Tunstah (not out) 2. extras 12 total 115 Parr'4 Bank : V 7 F Holme 11, Silcock 28,W 13 Broughall 1. 11 K Deane 2, J B Jackson 0, B Silcock 10, W Darbyshire not out 26, D Abbott 3. C Steen (not out) 21, extras 9, total (fur seven wickets) 111. PETER WALKER &amp; SON, LIMITED, 2ND XI. V. MOORE.- Played on the Brewery ground, Score: -</p>
        <p>BREWERY 2ND lir:. moonE. J Tomkhason b Foster ll Edwards b C)llins J Turner b Foster T Wright a Foster b Condliff, b Cotilos Banner T Collins run out 2 Johnson b Ashton W Ashton c Da.viisoa b Tnornton e Wright b Johnson l5 Collins W Johnson h Foster J Seed b Taylor J Bailey not out l9 Fleet c and b Aditon ...... 3 J Lloyd)) Taylor 0 Davidson c and b Ashton 1 It Knight lbw b Banner A Ross b Banner 3 Extras 4 Extras 1</p>
        <p>Total</p>
