      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>S FOLLOWS :-</head>
        <p>S FOLLOWS :-</p>
        <p>I To I Callers.</p>
        <p>7, a.m. 10-30 „ 10-45 „ 1.11-30 „ 1.11-45 } 12-0</p>
        <p>t 2-15 p.m.</p>
        <p>2-lb „ 1 3-15-,</p>
        <p>5-30 p.m.</p>
        <p>I 9-15,,</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON COUNTY COURT.</p>
        <p>THIMSDAY.—Before his Honour, Judge Wynne Ftoulkes.</p>
        <p>ALL ABltrr A TURKEY.—George Ravenscro:t cued Rebecca Locker, poultry dealer, Warrington, for 15s for a turkey allezed to have been sold by defendant for p7aintiff.—Mr Browne appeared for the defendant Blizlbeth Ann Ravens- croft,wite of the plaintiff, said she sold poultry in the market. On this particular occasion she left a turkey with a Mrs Longshaw. She handed it over to defendant, who had since said she sold the turkey but had not got the money for it.— Mr Browne remarked that defendant had never seen or heard of the turkey.—Blijah Place, market inspector, said he had seen the turkey on several occasioas on Mrs Ravens- croft's stall. (Laughter.) Subsequently Mrs Locker wanted to sell it to him for 841 per pound. She afterwards told him that she had sold the turkey to aMr and Mrs Grewirv. She asked him if be knew where they were, and he told her they were at Bedford Leigh. She said they had not paid her for her that he knew the turkey. It was 12 months lass Christ- mas since she was supposed to have had the turkey.—His Honour said he was not satisfied with the evidence against the defendant, and must non-suit plaintiff. That would not prevent her bringing action if she could offer additional evi- dence.</p>
        <p>CLAIM FOR WHISKEY AND Itinc—An action was brought en behalf of Messrs. Pattison, Limited, against Charles Car- rodus, landlord of the Green Dragon Hotel. Heatley, to re- cover £lO 7s sci for whiskey and rum delivered.—Mr. Rein- hardt, Birkenhead, appeared for the plaintiff, and Mr Browne represented the defendant.—Mr. Reinhardt explained that Messrs. Pattisoia were in liquidation, and :the action was taken through the liquidator. The two principal items in the bills were £6 5a lid on September 15th for whiskey. and £2 is. 6d. on December 7th for rum. The traveller who obtained the order was unfortunately not present, although he had been gi,Yen notice to attend. He had, however, a letter from defendant's solicitor, admitting the ord.r but saying that it was subsequently cancelled.— Mr. Browne said there had never been any delivery and the order was cancelled.—Mr. Reinhardt said the goods wer placed in bond.—His Honour: You cannot sue until they are delivered.—Mr. Reinhardt • We would have to wait three or four years for our accounts.—His Honour : You have no business to sell them unless they were ordered to be put in bond, which would Econstitute delivery. Mr. Browne says they were soot so ordered.—Kr. Reinhardt : I have not got the traveller and am unable to take the case further.— Mr. Browne said that if the whiskey was delivered his client was prepared to pay for it.—Mr Reinhardt : I cannot help myself. I accept the offer,—His Honour non-suited:plaintiff with costs.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON_ FIELD CLUB.</p>
        <p>_ The members of the Field Club mustered strongly on Saturdays near Burley Heyes, Appleton, and started thence, under the leadership of Mr. Whitley, to Stratton Moss, where a couple of hours were pleasantly and profitably spent in exploring and collecting. By the courtesy of the owners and ten- ants of the Moss and some adjoining lands, the members were enabled to see everything of inter- est to naturalists. The Moss itself has been much reduced in area by reclamation, and the existing portion seems to be altering in character as a result of better drainage of the district generally; heath plants apparently tending to supersede the earlier flora, though of this latter some interesting traces were noted. Of rare moss plants, the cladium mariscus, a sedge with bayonet-like leaves,_ and minute sharp teeth on the bayonet edges, keen enough to cut dee,ply the hand of the incautious botanist, was seen growing in some profusion. Polytrichum gracile, a large and handsome moss, was in full fruit;_ while the whole air of the Moss was fragrant with the sweet gale, which grow there very abundantly. The entomologists were less successful than the botanists; sore thousands of larva) were taken, but no exceptionally rare specimens were secured. Amongst spiders, the very rare male of epiblemum cingulatum was captured. In this species the females are common and plentiful, hut the male is very rarely seen this one being, in fact, the first yet taken near Virarrington. From the Mo,is the party were conducted to Wallspit House, Stretton, and there hospitably entertained by the genial leader and his family. After partaking of a tea, for which their scientific exertions had been an admir- able and effective preparation, the members su'ise- quently adjourned to the garden, and spent the evening either in admiring its beauties, playing bowls, or conversation, which was stimulated by various means tending to promote good fellowship. The Secretary subsequently apologised for the un- avoidable absence of the President, and thanked Mr Whitley for his kindness in enabling the members to visit and explore an ordinarily inaccessible col- lecting ground, and for his hospitality, which had restored the tired members to vigour and happi- ness. Mr. Whitley replied in felicitous terms, and the party bade adieu to their host and hostess about 8-30.</p>
      </div>
