      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1399. EARLESTOWN 8c NEWTON.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 16, 1399. EARLESTOWN 8c NEWTON.</p>
        <p>?Letters to the &lt;Maar.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS.</p>
        <p>Authors of letters intended_ for _publication must in at</p>
        <p>cases send their names and addre:ses to the Editor. Matters intended for publication must be written on one side only of the paper. Correspondents are strongly advised to write briefly. A short letter has much greater chance of insertion than a long one.</p>
        <p>POLICE CASES. At Earlestown Police Station on Monday morn- ing, before Mr. J. W. Emmett, Patrick Donoghue was charged with being drunk early the same morning, and in default of paying a Tine of 53 and costs was sent to gaol for 14 days. A SUCCESSFUL EARLESTOWN STUDENT. At the recent medical examinations held in con- nection with Victoria University, Rr. Peter Thomp- son, of Lowton, formerly of Earlestown,and Owen's College, succeeded in obtaining the degree of M.D., and was awarded Gold Medal. A SALVATION ARMY MAN DRUNK. At the Earlestown Police Station on Wednesday morning, before Mr. C. B. F. Barron, Roland Roth- well, a member of the Salvation Army, was charged with being drunk and disorderly in Bridge-street, Earlestown, on Titesday evening. He was fined 2s 6d isnd Casts. THE DROWNING OF A CHILD AT EARLESTOWN. The inquest on the body of the child, Joseph Norman College, who was drowned in a tub con- taining abont three inches of water on Wednesday afternoon last week, was held at the Town Hall, Ea rlestown,on 'Friday by Mr. S. Brighouee,coroner. The facts reported in our last issue were deposed to and a verdict of" Accidental death "was returned SUDDEN DEATH AT NEWTON. On Monday afternoon afternoon an old man named Hamlet Holcroft, 68 years of age, of Millt lane Newton employed by the Newton Urban Distric 'Council, was found dead, against a telegraph pole •nearthe Liverpool Farm School on the Winwick- a•oad. The deceased for three weeks past had beea suffering from heart disease, and this, coupled with the excessive heat, is supposed to have brought about his death. SUDDEN DEATH OF A CHILD. The death of a child 13 months old,named James S. Armstrong,infant son of James Armstrongst wag- on maker, of 17, Bank-street, Earlestown,was re- ported toithe police on Monday. The child died on the morning of the day named, without having been seen by a doctor. Dr. Latham was sent for, but the child died before he arrived, death being evidently due to convulsions. 1.0.G.T. HOPE OF EARLESTOWN LODGE. A meeting of the above was held in the Co-:Ter- ative Hall, Virargrave-road, on Monday. Previous to the adult meeting the juvenile temple met under the superintendency of Bro. Newman. Bro. J. Phillips, C.T., was in the chair and five candidates were admitted to membership. An interesting programme was given by Bro. Jones,Harold Roberts and Sisters Dunn and Jones, and cards of recita- tions given the members after which the temple was closed and the lodge was opened by Bro. Wil- liams, C.T. After the opening services Bro. Woods read a letter from the Norwood Lodge, Liverpool, regretting their inability to come on the date arranged owing to their open air work; also notice of conference to be held at Liverpool and Good Templars fete at Crystal Palace, London. Two candidates were admitted to membership, and the , violation committee reported visiting the Brother who had violated, but he refused to return. The re- port was accepted but they were asked to use their utmost endeavours to induce him to return. The lodge was then thrown open to visitors, and the fol- lowing programme was given. Sankey'a Hymn , "who is on the Lord's side," Bro. Singleton; recita- tion, Sister Francis; duet, Sisters Owen • concertina and mouth organ duet, Bros. Lsatherarrow ; ad- dress, Bro. G. Jones; seng, Bro. Phillips ; song. Bro. Stanley; mouth organ,Bro. J Wilson; Bro Lodge,of ; Ashton, addressed the meeting and the lodge was ; closed after voting 5s towards the purchase of games for next week's programme—a social night. SUNDAY SCHOOL'ADEMoNSTRATION AT EARLESTOWN. The annual demonstration of Sunday schools ' which should have been held on Whit-Mondey,was ' brought off under very favourable circumstances on Saturday afternoon, in brilliant weather. The schools taking part were the Welsh, Wesleyans, . Baptist, Salvation Army, Primitive Methodistssind k Brunswick Wesleyans. The scholars assembled at the various schools at one o'clock, and half an hour I later they mustered on the Market ground,making a brilliant show with their banners and banner- ettes. When the schools had been arranged three hymns were sung, to the accompaniment of the Viaduct band, Mr. T. Clare conducting. At the con- elusion of the last hymn, the schools filed off the market ground in the following order, each being led by its banner :—Earlestown Prize Band, Welsh ; Wesleyans, 50 scholars on the books, Mr R Owen, , superintendent, Mr Jones, secretary; Baptists, 200 scholars on the books, Mr E Evett, superintendent, , Mr J Morris, treasurer, Mr W Limond, secretary; Sevation Army with band,9o scholars on the books, Captain earle, Mr W Slephens, superintendent, Mr C Ilezeldine, treasurer; Primitive Methodists, 300 ' scholars on the books, Messrs Dickens and Austin, superintendents, Mr T Clarke, treasurer, Messrs J 11 ; Woods and J T Austin,secs; Sutton-road prize band: Earlestown Wesleyan, 500 scholars on the books; Is ; Messrs I' Clare, J H Profit, W Clare, George Clarke, ; end G W Warburton, superintendents, Mr P Chand- , ley, treasurer; and Mr T Parr, secretary. A short ; route was traversed, the procession proeeeding via Market-street, turning round the Vicarage into San- key street, Earle-street, Queen-street, Fairclough- streetsCross-lane,Bridge-streer. and Market-street to the Market ground. From here the schools dispersed to fields which had been kindly placed at their dis- pcsal. The Wesleyaus went to afield off St. Ilelens- road, lent by Mr. Alderson • the Welsh 'Wesleyans , to a field belonging to Mr. Berry; the Baptists to the Grange at Haydock ; the Primitive Methodists , to the Nine Arches farm; and the Salvation Army to Mrs. Pritchard's field off Wargrave-road. With the exception of the Welsh, and the Salvation Army, the scholars had tea served out te them in the field. Afterwards the evening was spent in various ways, racing, football, cricket. &amp;es being the chief forms of pastime until dusk.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>" HONOIJR TO WII3X HONOUR IS DUE."</head>
        <p>" HONOIJR TO WII3X HONOUR IS DUE."</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln answer to Mr. Windle'a letter, I beg to say that I have had an interview with the late teacher of the Y.M.C.A. &amp;hie Class held on Friday evenings, and he states emphatically that the class was in existence when the present Secretary entered upon his duties. So much for his statement "that no Spiritual work was being done during the week." Having proved my first statement I leave the matter and Shall not take any further notice of the correspond- ence, but remain,</p>
        <p>AN ON-LOOKER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>GOOD 'OLD WARRINGTON !</head>
        <p>GOOD 'OLD WARRINGTON !</p>
        <p>am very pleased to see that Warrington is not going to be out, done either by Liverpool or Manchester as far as music 'tor the peeple is concerned, for on Mon- thy eveninz last the Warrington League of the Crms brass band gave a special programme of dance music under their able conductor Mr. T. Hynes of Widnes, on the Corporation ground, Howley, and I an told that this is a start and many more of these performances will take place during the season.V,There were, as far as I •could judge between two and three thou- sand people present and they seemed to thoroughly en- joy thetnselves judging from the many who took part is the dances, but they would have enjoyed themselves better if a 'Coporation water cart had gone over the ground in the afternoon to lay the dust before the danc- ing commenced. Ido hope that some kind Corporation official will please take note and have this done when another band is giving a performance there. I think that this is a step taken in the right directioa and I for one must congratulate the committe of the Warrington Band Association and the Corporation for allowing the bands of the association to play in open spaces in various parts of the town. I believe the next performance given in open space will be given by the Volunteer Band on the Corporation ground Orford-lane on June, 14 under their old veteran conductor Mr. T. Armstrong.</p>
        <p>A LOVER OF MUSIC.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>CRICKET.</p>
        <p>The doings of the Warrington eleven must be noted though it is more pleasant to write of victories than de- feats. Warrington entertained Cheetham on Saturday but could not cheat 'em—excuse me !—of their victory. I humbly apologise for the weakness I have given way to, but lam only human and could not let the oppor- tunity pass. To pursue the subject further Why does a man inevitably succumb when tempted to pun? Women don't seem to be bent that way at all,but a man goes like a lamb to the slaughter or a novice to the wicket. It must be a strain of imbecility in our blood for which we are not accountable. However, I must re- vert to my definite sutject or someone will be accusinl me of trying to avoid writing of the exploite of Warring- ton and—er—the visitors.</p>
        <p>The homesters commenced the batting, the profess- ional and J. Callon going to the wicket. Both nsen played well and runs came steadily, the visitors being frequently applauded for smart fielding. The partner- ship was a profitable one and 54 runs were scored when Thewlis was raught at mid-off, his excellent batting having realized 28. One or two wickets fell for low scores before Callon was dismissed with 34 runs to his credit. E. C. Kendle, who WAS given a trial for the first time, succumbed to a grand ball from Parker. J. W. Bibby and T. Knowles stemmed the tide of disaster for a time, the latter compiling 22 and the former 14 runs. Whatever may have been expected from the remaining batsmen. nothing was done the innings closing for a total of 136. W. Allen, of the visitors,was in good form with the ball.</p>
        <p>The score required no great effort to beat, considering thescate of the wicket, and the capable eleven the visi- tors had brought. The Biethers Sinclair c pened the batting, fAcrd by Dr. Smith and Thewlis, and batted steadily, 30 runs being scored before a separation was effect ed. W. Parker played a vigorous game which im- parted confidence to the other tatters. When his score had reached 61, Kendle brought off a neat catch. Parker's innings included ten fours. W. H. Allen was sent back with 12 runs to his nanv,. and the visitors cloqed their innings for a total of 138 for"s wickets, E Collinge carrying out his bat for 24.</p>
        <p>Aicrharth was the scene of an encounter between Warrington and Liverpool on Tuesday afternoon. Toe visitors again had first innings. Callon and Thewlis croned the batting, and a disastrious start was made, the latter having to retire without opening his account. Dr. Smith filled his place and some steady batting wag seen. Captain Plorner succeeded Callon, who made 12 runs. The score rose steauily with two such capable men at the wicket and the game wore a better aspect. The Warrington captain was eventually caught out at deep point. Captain Plomer continues to bat in fine style and saw two or three changes at the wicket until W. M. Silcoock partnered him. The two,despit frequent changes in the bowling, augnaeated the score with what mubt have been irritating regularity to the home team, occasionally making brilliant strokes. The century was reached ; but all things have their end, and Silcock was caught for an invaluable 25.</p>
        <p>A few runs later Captain Plomer's long innings was closed,his score ot 74 including eleven boundary strekea. E. Woods and A. Duckworth contributed 11 rung each, the innings of the team closing for a total of 175.</p>
        <p>The Liverpudlians, who bad a particularly strong team, commenced their essay by sending H. B. Steel and W. B. St oddart to the wicket. The latter was brilliantly caught off Dr. Smith's first over. The batting gave some trouble after this, C. C. Pilkington, who succeeded, makidg a long stay. The Warrington aggregate was passed with four wickets in hand, Liverpool at the el-s 3 makino249. The best individual acores were: Pilking- ton, 73; Ould, 35; and Steel, 30. Warrington have thus had to submit to another deteat.</p>
        <p>Warrington and Cheethana second elevens engaged at Cheetbam. The game was a very close one and ended in a victory for the home team by six runs. C. R. Adamson for Warrington made 25 runs,and A. Thomsen 12 (not out). Farrington and Milsom made the best scores for the winners.</p>
        <p>Fairfield and St. Barnabas' on Saturday played on the ground of the former. The pitch was in fine condition and consEquently everything was favourable for a good match. The visitors were the first to bat, but their scoring was very weak, due to the strength of the home bowling, and when the score reached 16 the I, st wicket collapsed. The fielding of the Fairfi4d members was very sharp five of the players being caught out. Hudson was the only player who reached double figures, the homesters winning the game.</p>
        <p>After a pleasantly conteEt?d gam at Flartford,Sankey Wirn Mills defeated that team by 47 runs. Lave!! and Clarke fcr tbe visitors batted in grand style for their respective scores. Simpson bowled well, taking five wickets at a cost of 14 runs.</p>
        <p>Brick-street and Friars' Green S.S. played Monka,Hall and Co., at Lovely-lane, Brick•street winning'. For the home team Asnip and Wilcox bowled exceedingly well, the former takinE.Y. fire wickets for 12 and the latter fur for six. For the visitors Clare took five wickets for 18</p>
        <p>FrdAham inflicted a crushing defeat on Weaverham on Saturday. C. E. Linaker took five wie.kets for 24 and Wright 3 for 9. Rogers brought off a splPndid catch and H. N. Linaker is t 3 he complimentrd for smart field- ing. Boston and Kennerly played very useful innings.</p>
        <p>The Sankey Wesleyans lowered tha eqlours of Ulm Cheshire Lines eleven on Saturday. Cotterill, Light,and Clare reached double figures for the winners, not one of the railway men, who are always determined opporeats, attaining to that dignity.</p>
        <p>Fearnhead will not readily forget the slatinz they re- ceived at the hands of the Post Office. Gardiner for a total of 49. batted in grand style for ths winners. :Fraser compiled 23 for Fearabead.</p>
        <p>The Warrinzton Catholics had th e beat of a name with Richmond's on Saturday. Bate topped the loaning E cores with 29 runs to his credit for the winners.</p>
        <p>The second eleven of Messrs. Peter Walker and Son defeated Moore representatives without difficulty. Achton, and Tomkinson bitted well for the Brewery,Johnson only reaching double figurcs for Moore. -- Wanington Police journeyed to Preston on Tuesday and were deffat.d by the police team of the town. HankinFon, for the winners,tracle a score or 30. Newev'q score of 15 not out, was the best on the Warrington Ode, which does not say much considering the hitting powers of their eleven.</p>
        <p>CYCLING.</p>
        <p>That promising rider, W. Collin, of Warrington, seems to experience no difficulty in winning fresh laurels, for at the athletic festival, promoted by the Lowerhouse Cricket Club, on Saturday, he was successful in taking first prizes in the one mile and two miles bicycle handi- caps.</p>
        <p>- J. BAYLEY IsLie pays Spada! Care to:the Quality and Cleanliness of Goods he makes.—Advt. PUBLIC BENEFIT UNDER THE CLOCK POE BEST BOOTS IN</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>SERIOUS ACCIDENT TO A. Sr. HELENS BUILDER.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night, Mr. Fred Brown, builder and con- tractor, of N')rth-read, St. Helens, was knocked down by a lurry belonging to Mr. Lyon, mineral water maker. The horse's hoqs struck and lacerated both his hands and arms.and the front wheel of the lurry went over his chest. He was then pnsbed Wong tits road some dis- tance by the back wheel, which finally went over his body, causing him excruciating pain. Two friends who were with him took him to Dr. Cooke's surgery, and he was afterward onveyed home. One of his ribs was force-1 out of position, and his legs and body generally were badly crushed.</p>
        <p>A CHILD BURNT TO DEATH</p>
        <p>Mr. Brighouse, county coroner, held an inquest at the Railway Hotel, 'Matto Heath. St. Helens, on Saturday. on the body of Richard, the fourteen months old son of John Yates Barton, of 62, Elephant-lane. who died from burns. Mrs. Barton said that on Wednesday she lit the fire in the front place, and put the box of matches on the table in the kitchen, where the little boy was playing. She went across the street for a few minutes to a nE ighbour's, and on her return' found the child in flames. The matches were F.trewn abDut the flor, and it was eyidrnt the child had reached them off the table awl struck several petting his clothes on fire.—Mrs. Ann Parr spoke to nutting out the flames and sending for Dr. Gray.—The jury returned a verdict of " Accidentally burned."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>HOICT FATALITY TO A BOY.</head>
        <p>HOICT FATALITY TO A BOY.</p>
        <p>Mr. Coroner Brithouse held an ingurst at the St. 'Helens Town Hall on Tuesday on the body of Joseph Burke, aged 14, of 13, Parr street, St. Helens, who died in the Provinence Hospital on Saturday night from injuries received the previous Thursuay.—Martin Burke, the lad's father, said the lad was working along with him on Thursday. They were ra-olating, the top of Mr. whittamnre's coaching establishment in Harlock•street. The lad at the tims of the accident was at the top of the hoist on the top floor of the bailding. By some means he sliProd and fell down the hoist. h:s head striking the first floor, and then fell on the eige of the waggon, which threw him forward on his head on to the ground. —George Prrr, of 69, Newcross-street, a plumber's apprentice, tor Mr. Brown, said he was on the top floor with Burke. A numblr of sacks of corn were lying as the floor, and Burke said he would jump over them. He went to the edge of the hoist to get a run, and took hod of the ladder standing up front the hoist. The ladder was not fast, and when the decsased took hold of it to get a spring, the ladder slipped and he fell back- wards down the hoist.—The jury returned a verdict of Accidental death.'</p>
        <p>GOLF.</p>
        <p>MERSEY CLUB.</p>
        <p>The followina egrds were returned on Saturday in the Monthly Medal Competition for the Captain'ts Prize.</p>
        <p>Gross Holes. Net. Bernard Williamson Halton Dr. Murray Dr Peacock e .Tames Falrelough J B joynson Fowler Joynson M ac g r e g o r</p>
        <p>SllROPSHlRE.—Comfortable APARTAINNTS for faixiilre-s wishing to spend holidays in this beautiful county; laege house, beautiful lawn.—WARNuR HOUSE, Newport, Shropshire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>TWO ATTEMPTED SUICIDES AT ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>TWO ATTEMPTED SUICIDES AT ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Police Court, on Tuesday,b4ore Mr. W. J. Thompson and Lieutenant-Colonel M.'Tear, Lionel , Swift, aged twenty-one, of 43, Clyde-streeet, was brought up on remand charged with attempted suicide. —Chief Constable Wood stated that between tcn and eleven o'clock on the evening of Monday, the sth inst., the prisoner, his father, and sister were in the kitchen, preparing for bed, when the prisoner went into the back kitchen. Shartly after the father heard a noise, and on turning round saw his son standing in the doorway with a knife in his band. He drew the knife across his throat cut it, and made it bleed. Sergeant Stewart was sent for, and prisoner said "I am tired of living : I wish they had let me finish it." He was brought before the magistrates last Tuesday, and remanded for a week. Swift now promised he would never do it again. His father was in court, and agreed to look after him.—On this condition the bench discharged prisoner. Sarah Boardman, a widow, of 85, Lyon-street, was also chargel with attempted suicide.—Chief Constable Wood said that from what he was told. Constable Wilkes went to the house of prisoner at a quarter to nine on Tuesday morning, and charged her with having at- tempted to commit suicide on Monday night by throw- ing h(reelf into a dam of water at the Ravenhead Sand- beds. She said, "I didn't know what I was doing; I had some words with my daughter." He brought her to the Town Hall, where, on her removal to the cell, she said, "I threw myself into the water through a bit of trouble with my daughter. A man named Sharrack pulled me out of the water, or I should have drowned ; nothing could have saved me."—Evidence wee given by Wilkes.—Prisoner said she did not intmd to take away her life. She had nine children, and was going to be married again in Auzust ; and on the prospective husband promising to take charge of her, ehe was die- charged.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>SUICIDE AT EARLESTOWN.</head>
        <p>SUICIDE AT EARLESTOWN.</p>
        <p>On Sunday morning the body of William Henry Laminas, 47 years of age, of 7, Rathbone-s!rset, &amp;ales- towm. a machine man employed at the Viaduct Works, was taken from the Sankey Canal. About half past nine a canal ranger named Joseph Prime, found a cap and vest on the towing path, near Bridge's farm, and judging the owner must be in the water, he informed P.C. Schofield. who, with Sergeant Banks, dragged the canal, and at 11.50 brought the body of Lammas to the surface,and it was conveyed home. It appears Lammas had been drinking during the week and had absented himself from his work. About 7.45 on Sunday morning h? left home,and waa not seen again until his dead body was taken from the canal.</p>
        <p>The inquest was held at the Town Hall, on Tuesday afternoon by Mr. A. Brighouse. . _ . . . _</p>
        <p>Mary Lariirnas said the deceased was her husband,and was 37 years of age. She last saw him alive at 7-45 on Sunday morning when he gave one of the children three- pence and went out saying he was going for a walk. Re bad been away from his work a week, drinking, but she had no suspicion that he intended taking his life. Joseph Prime,a canal ranger employed on the Sankey Canal,said that about half-past nine on Sunday morning -be found a cap and vest on the towing path. The cap was on the vest, and in the cap was a penny, a piece of tobacco and a knife. He took the articles to the police and informed them of the circumstances under which they had been Lund. P.C. Schofield said about 10 o'clock on Sunday morn- ing, the previous witness brought a cap and vest to his house, and he and Sergeant Banks dragged the canal, and found the body of the deceased about 11-50,</p>
        <p>A verdict of "suicide whilst of unsound mind" was re- turned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>OUR LOCAL caußcaEs.</head>
        <p>OUR LOCAL caußcaEs.</p>
        <p>CAIRO-S rREET CHAPEL.</p>
        <p>Special services were held morning and evening at this church on Sundiy, when the Rev. W. H. Drummond preached on "Social Sympathy "and "An Apostle of Charity." Collections were taken on behalf of the War- rinuton District Nursing Association. LYSIIII WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>The 19th anniversary of the Lyn-1m Wesleyan Sundey School was celebrated on Sunday, when the preacher in the morning and evening was the Rev. H. G. Roberts of Warrington. In the afternoon a sacred cantata entitled "Cloud and Sunshine ;or The return of Israel," was given bv the choir and scholars, assisted by the Misses Davies (llolinfare), and Mr. R. Carbett, of Warrington. Other solos and duets were taken by the Misses vs7:flit an- ley and Warburton, and Meesrs. A. B. Ferris, junior, Percy Ferris, J. Lord, William Hinton, and Thomas Warburton. Mr. J. Upson was the conductor, and Mr. Henry Browne presided at the harmonium. On Monday the usual treat to the scholars took place. GOSPEL HALL, FORSTER-SrREET,,ORFORD-L ANE. On Sunday, June Ilth, Mr. A. C. Ashworth. South- port,conducted the service,speaking from Gen. 49-5. :-!ts said that the characters of Simeon and Levi,wcre breth- ren naturally and brethren in crira?. They plotted and carried out one of the most wicked deeds recorded in the Bible and to do it they used one of God's holy ordin- ances, that of Circumcision. How like men to-lay who use the things of God as a cloak for their wickedness. Yet we find that Levi's posterity was afterwards taken up by God te act as His priests for the Israelities, show- in 4 how God can take up even the worst of people and make them trophies of His grace. The Bible is not only the history of the world but also the history of God's dealings with man. He first dealt with man in inno- cency. When man failed He gave him the promise of Christ coming. He again tried man after the flood but each stage man grew worse till at last they put to death the ssn of God. We now liva in the day of God's grace, the most privileged age. This will be followed by day of Judgment. Now salvation is offered freely by a living Christ in the heavens to whosoever will. He will shortly come for those who have believed in Him and the door being shut the unsaved will be shut out.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>U RAPPIIN HALL.</head>
        <p>U RAPPIIN HALL.</p>
        <p>THE ALLEGED SACRILEGE AT THE CHURCH.—John Daley (33) was brought up at the Warrington Occasional Court, on Tuesday, before Mr. H. Neile, charged on re- mand with having on the 30th May committed a felony by breaking open the offertory boxes in Grappenhall Church and stealing the contents. He was further charged with breaking out of the church. Some .formal evidence was given and the prisoner was again re- manded, this time until the 22nd June,to give the police an opportunity of enquiring into his previous history. Colonel Hammersley, Chief Constable of Cheshir2, was present.</p>
        <p>A SUCCESSFUL LIvELIPOOIT CITY GOUNCILLOR. —As a tragesman who understands the knotty questions which concern the Liverpool public, Councillor Henry Miles has received spocial attention from the monthly mag,,a- eine "The British Warehouseman." His portrait, show. in him hale and hearty, is connected with a very full description of a noted business career. We learn that Councillor Henry Miles has several large establishments in Church-street, where ho conducts an extensive busi- ness in millinery and fancy eonds. Be commenced to trade on his own account in 1889, and success has fol- lowed his movements, for he now actively directs the affairs of three establishments, and doss not neglect his duties as the civic representative for St. Peter's Ward in tee Liverpool City Conseil. It seems that after his five years' apprenticeship to a Liverpool draper he became a bayer,and wag for:six years and:a hslf bead of the hosiery department at Woolright'e. In 1880 he became glove and hosiery buyer for Owen Owen, Lendon-road, while nine years later he embarked in business on his own account. With a capital of only £550 he took a shop, opposite the pro-Cathedral, rented at £6OO a year, and rated and taxed to the amount of £2OO. However, the £BOO a rear rent and taxes did not deter him, Ho spelt £5O on fixtures and fittings, and re erved £5OO for stock. Only ten years have elapsed, and now his glove depaitmsnt is described as the largest out of London.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1899-06-16_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>WARRINGTON MARKETS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON MARKETS.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Corn Market on Wedreday new wheat (white) fetched 4s Od (701 b. to the bushel) ; red wheat, 3s lid ; new oats, 2s 6d. Flour best seconds, per 2401 b., 18s 6d; Bran, lid to is lid. per 201 b. Prices at the Provision Market were as follows:—Fresh butter is to is 21 per lb.; fresh eggs, 11 and 12 for is; fowls, 5s to 6s per couple; English beef, 5d to 10d; lamb, 9d to is Id; mutton 6d to 10d; veal, 8d to 10d; pork, 6d to Bd. A.-4K fcr CUNNINGHAM'S HIGH-CLASS MINERAL WATEIRS.—Advt.</p>
