        <p>A MUSICAL COMPANY'S RAILWAY EXPERIENCE AT Nrc-N.RRINGTON. SUUNTED ON A SIDING FOR THREF. HOURS. "'IE PUBLIC BOUND IN FETTERS." In the Qaeen's Bench Disesienal Court, on Saturday before Just ices Dip and Lawrence, the appeal case of Driver v. the London and North-15v astern RaLway Corn- pacy, came on for Bearing. Mr. Watt said tie appellant was proprietor of a musical touring company, and in February Let he and his company were touring in Lan- cashire and Derbyshire. After having completed an en- gagement at Present-, in Lancashire, they started for Tideswell, in Derbyshire, where they were advertised to perform on Fe btu try 23rd. They travelled by the Lendon and Nerth-Western Railway, their destination being Buxton. Their route was to be by the London and North-Western, and then by the Midland to Miller's Dale, which was about two miles from Tioeswell. A special carriage was provided for them by the train leaving Prescot at 9-30 a.m. At Warrington Junction the carriage was unoccupied so that it might be attached te another train, and it was then shunted on to a siding, where it remained " high and dry" tor three hours. (Laughter.) As a conse- quence they missed the Buxton train to which their carriage ought to have been coupled,and they ultimately reached Tide swell at half-past niue o'clock in the even- ing, too late to give their performance. Mr. Driver brought an action in the Bho:nsbury County Court to recover damages, but the judge held that by the condi- tions printed on the tickets issued by the railway com- pany they had contracted themselves out of liability for negligence on the part of the company's servants. One of the conditions was that the company was not liable "for an, loss, danasge,iujury,delay, or detention caused or arising off their railway." Mr. Justize Day : Bat this was on their railway at Warrington Junction. Mr. Wet-, said that was so. There was another con- dition on woieh the defend int company relied, and it was announced ia their time-table, to the aect that the Company did not; undettake that the trains should start or arrive at the times advertised, and that they would not be accountable for any loss which might arise from detention or delay to trains by which the plaintiff and his company travelled. Counsel submitted that this condition did not cover the case of passengers who were negligently shunted on to a siding and kept there for three hours. Bad they not complained, they migh have been there yet (laighter), and he submltted that hey were entitled to a new trial. Mr. Shearman, for the defendant company, said the point had been decided over and over again in a long series of cases. The facts had not been quite correctly stated by his learned friend. The plaintiff's company, with their scenery, left Prescet to go to Buxton by the London and North-Weston Railway• They had to change at St. Helens, change again at IVarrington,again at Broadheath, and again at St3ckportawhere they were to be hitched on to a train early in the morning. The train arrived at Warrington at 10 41, at what was known as the High Level Station. Ordinary passengers could walk down the steps in order to get into the train which stared for Breadheath from a lower level ; but as the company had scenery which could not be taken down the steps, they were taken along the line and put on to a siding. The time-table showed tbat there was no other train from Warrington to Broad- heath between 10.41 a m. and 1 14 p.m., and the corn- pany,with their scenery,had to wait until they could be attached to the later train. He could not conceive that there was any great grievance in being put on a siding. If they had been put en the main line perhaps no action cou'd have been brought by the present parties, but only, perhaps, by their executors—(laughter.) He cited a number of cases in supporo of his contention that the defendant company was not liable. Mr. Watts said the cases cited did not apply to pas- sengers taken away in a carriage from a train and put on a siding for hours. Mr. Justice Day said the railway company,by the con- ditions laid down in their time-tables and on their tickets, protected themselves from the result of their ser- vants' negligence. He was sorry,but the list of decisions ! was too strong for the plaintiffs. Railway companies appeared to have bound the public in fetters. The court, however, could not help that, as they were allowed to do se by Act of Parliament. The appeal must be dismissed with costs. Mr. Justice Lawrence concurred. Mr. Watts asked for leave to appeal b the higher court, seeing that the case was one of great importance Ito the travelling public. Mr. Justice Day said they would grant leave but they had no doubt as t 3 the soundness of their judgment— (hear, hear). .9. THE FRIENDS OF ADAM. Some new and interesting discoveries have been made recently which shed additional light on prehistoric times, and give us an inkling of the state of affairs that existed in the days when Adam lived -Days that are separated from the present century by millions of years. The discoveries relate to the animal kingdom of that period. The elephant of to-day sinks into in- significance beside the mighty creatures of the form- er ages. One of the animals recently discovered was 801t1ong. Toe largest of the animals was the canarrisaurus dinosaur, which weighed about , 120,000111. i., was 16 ft. high at its hip and 80ft in ' length. This huge creature was one of the great family of dinosaur:s,a species of tremendous lizards. Its bones were found in a bed of rock of the period geologi- cally called the jurassic. jHad the catnarasaurus dinosaur stepped on an animal as big as a horse— or as small—it would have crushed it. The camerasaurus dinosaur was also one of the mighty lizards. Dinosaur is the family name and comes from the Greek demos— terrible, mighty—and sauros—a lizard. The camera jin his first name, is the Greek kamara, a vaulted ' chamber, and he is so callei ecause of the shape of his body. In truth he's well named for another reason—his body is roomier than many a chamber in which men live and die in this world The scientists who have studied the camarasaurus with so much diligence, with such profound interest, will tell you that this tremendous creature was Soft, long "over all" his head was 2ft. long, his neck 15t. long, his body 231 t. long, his tail 40ft. long. From foot to knee joint his legs were 6ft. long ; from foot to hip joint 16ft long. He weighed over 50 tons. He lived to be 500 years old. His front teeth were Gins, long. His appetite was literally insatiable; he ate every wak- ing minute. Yet some scientists believe he and his kind starved to death. His food was vegetable— nothing else. He was amphibious, and roamed over the western part of North America. He was peaceful in disposition and habit, but he could put up a stiff fight. By his side a man would look as big as a fly beside a pug dog, the largest elephant would look as big as a terrier beside a horse. An ordinary car- riage horse weighs about I,loolbs. ; this monstrous lizard about as much as 105 carriage horses. Excavation is very hard and troublesome work. The discovery of a fossil bone is but the first step of years of hard and elaborate labour. The earth is carefully removed from around the fragments. As each portion is removed it is carefully saturated with a kina of One which firmly holds the broken pieces of bone and prevents their loss. Then plas- ter of paris is freely used to further bind together the pieces until, secure the bones can be lifted from h teir bed of millions Of years. They are then en- cased in plaster of paris, bound with strips of raw hide, put in crates, and shipped to the museum. There the plaster Gf paris is chipped away as nicely as the sculptor rounds his finest lines, until the bones are clean and clear, Then the esteolo-, gists take them in hand and build up the skeleton and fit piec. by piece in its proper place.— cience</p>
