        <p>THE ABERDEEN CEMETERY SCANDAL. OVER A HUNDRED GRAVES OPENED. Within the last three days over a hundred graves have been opened in Nelifield Cemetery, Aberdeen. The greatest consternation has been caused by the dis- closures, and yesterday several special gangs of diggers corn enced work opening more of the graves. The work will go on tor several weeks. THE WISH OF A PUGILIST'S FATHER, NEW YORK,THURSDAY. "Jim will keep up this devilment until he is licked. I hop , he will get licked soon, and then he will come to salvation." The abeve is an extract from a discourse preached by the father of Jeffries, the pugilist, in the streets of Los Angeles (California). EXTRAORDINARY DEATH OF A ST. HELENS WOMAN. Clara Howard, aged thirty-seven, wife of Robert Howard, joiner, of 59, Argyle-street, St. Helens, was found dead in the cellar of her house yesterday morning under most extraordinary circumstances. The husband stated that at half-past eleven o'clock on Wednesday night he went to bed, leaving the deceased sitting in the kitchen, apparently in her usual health. She had for some time been in the habit of taking quantities of chlorodyne. He saw teething furthur of her, as she had for some time slept in the back room with the children., At half-past six yerterday morning he found his wife dead and co'd at the bottom of the cellar steps. Her bead was injured and was lying in a pool of blood. The coroner has been communicated with. THE GREENFIELD SENSATION. CARSON DISCHARGED. George Wil lam Carson was yesterday morning charged in custody, at Saddleworth, with caus'ng the death of his father, George Carson, by kicking him on the 7th inst., at Greenfield, The evidence was a repiti- tion of that given at the inquest. At the close the Chair- man said there was not sufficient evidence to bring the charge home to the prisoner. ' °MINTY COURT JURYMEN FINED. His Honour Judge Parry, at the Salford County Court yesterday, fined John Henry Clark and John Chapman each £5 for failing to appear as jurymen after being summoned. ARMED BURGLARS IN BIRMINGHAM. During the early hours of yesterday morning a man named Bowyer, living in Vaughton-street, Birmingham, was disturbed by a couple of burglars who had entered his house. As soon as he got up the men retreated, but he followed them outside, and one of the thieves turned and fired a revolver at him. The bullet strnck him in the left leg and he fell. The burglars escaped. LATEST LOCAL WEATHER FORECAST. The weather forecast for the twenty-four hours end- ing noon tc-day is as follows :—Variab,e or southerly airs. Fine, warm. CASTE RIOTB IN MADRAS. MANY RIOTERS KILLED. , An Exchange Company's Allahabad telegram, yester- day, states that one British and two native regiments are engaged in quelling the caste conflicts in Madras,and the situation which was latterly so grave is row reassur- ing. A great number of rioters have been killed and wounded, and about a hundred arrests have been made. THE FRENCH MINISTERIAL CRISIS. PARIS, THURSDAY. M. Poincare this morning informed the President officially that he accepted the task of fo-ming a Cacinet, though the distribution of portfolios is otherwise un- certain. M. Poincare intends te reserve for himself the Ministry of War. YESTERDAY'S CRICKET. THE 2ND TEST MATCH. ENGLAND v AUSTRALIA.. The second test match between England and Australia was commenced at Lord's yesterday in the presence of 20,000 spectators. Maclaren won the toss and the Eng- lishmen started batting. hut made a disastrous start, five wickets going down for 45 runs. An excellent stand by F S Jackson and G L Jessop brightened the prospect and ultimately the English total reached 206. The Australians started none too well, It sing 3 wickets for 69 runs. The following is the scare up to close of play : ENGLAND. A C MaaLaren b Jones C B Fry c Trumble b Jones K S Ranjitsinhji c and b Jones — 8 C L Townsend at Kelly b Howell F S Jielcson b Jones Hayward b Noble Tyldesley c Darling b Jones G L Jessop c Trumper b Trumble Lilley not out Mead b Jones Rhodes b Jones Extras 9 Total AUSTRALIANS. J J Darling c Ranjitsinhji b Rhodes J Worrell c Haywird b Rhodes lB C Hill not out 72 S R Gregory c Lillev b Jessop 31 A Noble, not out Total for three wickets 156 LANCASHIRE v. HAMPSHlRE.—Lancashire, 163; Hampshire 163 for 3 wickets. LEICESTERSHIRE v. YORKSHlRE.—Leicesterehire, all out, 295.</p>
